window.GUIDELINES = {"categories":[{"id":"DV","name":"Development","short":"Development"},{"id":"DP","name":"Design & Planning","short":"Design & Planning"},{"id":"QR","name":"Quality & Risk Management","short":"Quality & Risk"},{"id":"IO","name":"Infrastructure & Operations","short":"Infrastructure & Ops"},{"id":"MW","name":"Meta-work","short":"Meta-work"},{"id":"CC","name":"Cross-cutting","short":"Cross-cutting"}],"guidelines":[{"id":"DV1","category":"DV","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to automate routine, verifiable development tasks under human oversight; demand AI to produce reviewable steps and plan of action.","scenarios":["Coding: A developer asks AI to write the boilerplate for a new API endpoint. AI first lists the files it will touch and its plan; the developer approves, then reviews the code and writes tests for what AI wrote before it's merged.","Debugging: A developer points AI at a failing test and asks it to fix the bug. AI explains what it thinks is broken and the fix it plans, makes the change, and the developer checks it before shipping.","Refactoring: A developer asks AI to rename a function everywhere it's used. AI shows the full list of spots it will change first, so the developer can confirm nothing unexpected is swept in."],"origins":["Coding/Programming: Automate well-scoped, verifiable, rote coding tasks, with human review.","Bug Fixing/Debugging: Automate well-scoped, verifiable debugging sub-tasks, no final changes.","Refactoring & Maintenance: Automate routine, well-scoped, verifiable refactoring and maintenance tasks."],"table_practices":["Automate recurring upkeep such as log parsing, CI, and data updates","Delegate mechanical refactors that you dictate and can verify","Encode repeated work as checked-in skills, instructions, or custom agents","Orchestrate specialized agents that hand off work and request clarification","Have AI refine plans before authorizing implementation","Have AI give pull requests a first review while retaining human approval"],"practices":[{"name":"Automate recurring upkeep such as log parsing, CI, and data updates","definition":"AI takes over repeated maintenance chores, including regression-log parsing, work-item drafting, CI/CD iteration, and routine data updates.","includes":"Concrete recurring operational work formerly done by hand.","excludes":"Diagnosing a particular failure.","quotes":[{"text":"Doing a first pass to update any pull requests I own following comments I received on it.","who":"MS11"},{"text":"I want to know how to use AI for architectural diagrams and understand pros and cons of the design","who":"MS145"},{"text":"I use mcp to queue a pipeline run, which lets me configure a lot of parameters just by providing a semantic prompt these are really helpful","who":"MS23"},{"text":"'- quickly catch up the context on a project in Q&A manner - design prototypes with heavy human in the loop - automate & iterate CI and CD for prod","who":"MS35"},{"text":"it would also good if can have AI analyze the efficacy of CI/CD and suggest improvement which can be done. For instance, if CI/CD pipeline takes X amount of time and AI can review last Y runs and suggest improving execution time.","who":"MS149"},{"text":"I am unsure about the stakeholder's question. If stakeholders are internal, I think have agentic workflow provide communications on release status would be a nice to have. Though, I would not want to have agents making emergency decisions. Those should always route to a human.","who":"MS14"},{"text":"I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like generating test cases from a spec, parsing regression logs, and drafting ADO work items — but I keep it in human-reviewable increments: it proposes a plan and reviewable diffs, and I sign off before anything is applied or pushed. For anything security- or performance-critical, I make the final call myself rather than letting the AI finalize it.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"watch me work and update my kanban boards","who":"MS64"},{"text":"Currently using preparing documents, emails sometimes, training materials, ppt, etc","who":"MS177"}]},{"name":"Delegate mechanical refactors that you dictate and can verify","definition":"AI performs large but straightforward code changes, such as rename sweeps or dictated refactors, that the developer can personally check.","includes":"Mechanical code transformations with clear expected results.","excludes":"Generating tests and mixed routine artifacts.","quotes":[{"text":"- Refactoring the large module - Bug fixes - performance improvement","who":"MS214"},{"text":"Use AI for repetitive tasks on known patterns. AI-powered code completion.","who":"PF25"},{"text":"A glorified grep replace across the codebase is helpful and has been used in my development.","who":"MS26"},{"text":"The primary use cases I have for AI in my daily work-life: (a) reviewing my code, (b) talking through design decisions and trade-offs, (c) debugging non-trivial errors, (d) performing extensive-but-straightforward refactorings that I dictate and can personally verify, (e) writing unit tests that I can review.","who":"MS30"},{"text":"Automate repetitive tasks, review and finalize completed units.","who":"PF8"}]},{"name":"Encode repeated work as checked-in skills, instructions, or custom agents","definition":"Developers turn recurring tasks into reusable skills, context or instruction files, harnesses, or custom agents, often stored with the repository, so later runs perform the work consistently.","includes":"Creating or checking in skills, instruction files, harnesses, or custom agents for recurring work.","excludes":"Embedding the guideline set itself as guidance.","quotes":[{"text":"I create my own agents to do these on demand. I also do data scraping for on demand analysis.","who":"MS116"},{"text":"I usually use AI to understand the issue and create a draft PR with the fix. If there is any scope for routine & repeat work, I usually create a skill and check it into the repo.","who":"MS23"}]},{"name":"Orchestrate specialized agents that hand off work and request clarification","definition":"Several agents take distinct roles, exchange work across phases, and bring the developer in at points that need clarification.","includes":"Multi-agent workflows with defined roles, handoffs, and targeted human input.","excludes":"A single reviewing agent.","quotes":[{"text":"I'm currently working with several bundled agent+resumable-session+sidecar-file agent personas who break up work concerns across things like 'test-design' 'engineer-system' 'tools' or 'dev-box' and I get them to exchange information, find meaningful places for me to provide input, and hand off between investigation-dev-test-review so that I can spend my time clarifying important things instead of personally deploying and debugging.","who":"MS27"},{"text":"Working with AI to break the whole assignment into sub tasks, review together those tasks, approve\\require changes, ask to implement, make local review together and approve\\ask for changes, create a PR, using separate dedicated agent to run deep review while the dev agent to monitor for PR comment, review together, address and comment back. Monitor for the PR to be merged, deployed in to Test and trigger verification steps and providing final report. Iterate based on the needs.","who":"MS85"}]},{"name":"Have AI refine plans before authorizing implementation","definition":"AI drafts a plan, specification, or task breakdown; the developer corrects it until the scope is solid and only then permits implementation.","includes":"A reviewed plan or specification explicitly precedes and bounds implementation.","excludes":"Exploring alternatives without an implementation handoff.","quotes":[{"text":"I feel like we do most those things already in some capacity by having it create plans in markdown for review or in chat directly. Code is then AI and human reviewed to ensure quality.","who":"MS39"},{"text":"research and teach me about data and performance on a program, onboard me, plan for increasing the data we log, feed data to AI, research how to optimize with additional data the program, implement","who":"PF15"},{"text":"give AI a task directly and see if it can figure out itself by agents.md and other docs already there. if the design has some issue, will point out and change direction and then go to implementation phase.","who":"MS92"},{"text":"Again, already doing all of these in development work. Using skills like superpowers brainstorm skill you can work through planning and implementation phasing with human review and understanding crucial to the process.","who":"MS130"},{"text":"Collaboratively developing a strategy/infrastructure for a given project with AI. Then using AI to produce content, but for one section of this strategy at a time with back and forth testing, validation and iteration of AI outputs","who":"PF23"},{"text":"I would use AI to investigate unfamiliar code, trace relevant dependencies, and propose a small implementation plan before making changes. I would then have it implement the work in reviewable increments, with targeted tests and explicit human approval for security-sensitive, performance-critical, or broadly consequential changes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I would say that most of this here is already in use. I use LLMs to generate plans and specs for work to be done. This helps define a specific scope that agents can adhere to (but they still drift at times). I have used LLMs to review large code bases to understand potential bugs and gain an understanding more quickly of complex build systems.","who":"MS14"},{"text":"I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like generating test cases from a spec, parsing regression logs, and drafting ADO work items — but I keep it in human-reviewable increments: it proposes a plan and reviewable diffs, and I sign off before anything is applied or pushed. For anything security- or performance-critical, I make the final call myself rather than letting the AI finalize it.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"i truly wish the build plan phases are better distributed. there should be a command or something that can be flagged that will allow one to programaticaly allow a planner model and builder seperate model. we can do it now, but i have only found a way to do it via logic commands. its important because the gate you can create ebtween the phases gives you a higher confidence level when scopping and coding.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"Working with AI to break the whole assignment into sub tasks, review together those tasks, approve\\require changes, ask to implement, make local review together and approve\\ask for changes, create a PR, using separate dedicated agent to run deep review while the dev agent to monitor for PR comment, review together, address and comment back. Monitor for the PR to be merged, deployed in to Test and trigger verification steps and providing final report. Iterate based on the needs.","who":"MS85"}]},{"name":"Have AI give pull requests a first review while retaining human approval","definition":"AI inspects code, diffs, or pull requests and reports findings, while the developer still performs the full review and decides approval.","includes":"AI-assisted review that remains an input to a human review.","excludes":"A separate reviewer placed before the team.","quotes":[{"text":"Use copilot for majority of the coding, code review. Use agent that driven by documentation for [incident system] investigation, bug fixing","who":"MS164"},{"text":"I'm using AI to help me review our pull requests, but I will always do a full review myself and use my knowledge to judge if the PR can be approved or not.","who":"MS11"},{"text":"the skills like /review and /security-review have been very helpful in this scenario. Also the mcp servers like kusto are cherry on top for debugging purpose.","who":"MS101"},{"text":"I currently use AI to find and synthesize data from multiple sources. An example from today: \"How does kubectl implement the deployment of a template from a URL? Look at the source code and tell me if anything in the kubectl implementation suggests changes to this PR we are reviewing.\"","who":"MS52"},{"text":"The primary use cases I have for AI in my daily work-life: (a) reviewing my code, (b) talking through design decisions and trade-offs, (c) debugging non-trivial errors, (d) performing extensive-but-straightforward refactorings that I dictate and can personally verify, (e) writing unit tests that I can review.","who":"MS30"},{"text":"Using AI to understand parts of a codebase, or to review PRs, has already been really helpful. The one about source-linked explanations and recommendations sounds great, though in my experience, it still requires some amount of healthy skepticism since sometimes the conclusions/recommendations trace back to some flaw in the reasoning chain.","who":"MS8"},{"text":"I would, and currently do, use all of the above in my development work. I do a lot of 'pair programming' with an agent, where we will discuss the changes I want to make and either it provides the code that I then review and edit into the source files (or ask questions about), or I make the changes and it reviews them and points out any issues it sees in my code.","who":"MS154"},{"text":"I often find myself asking AI to review unstaged changes and report its findings back to me so that I can look them over and approve any proposed changes. When investigating a bug, I will also ask AI to help me conduct an initial investigation first to guide me and help me understand the affected code paths better, especially if the bug is in an area unfamiliar to me.","who":"MS200"},{"text":"code review, deep dive, design and coding.","who":"MS42"},{"text":"I always take in count the changes proposed by AI in PRs","who":"MS124"}]}]},{"id":"DV2","category":"DV","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to find and synthesize context from multiple sources to make changes with expected trade-offs; demand AI to follow standards and disclose scope and assumptions.","scenarios":["Coding: A developer asks AI to add a new field to a data model. Before writing anything, AI reads the model, the API layer, and the tests that touch it, then makes the change consistently across all three: telling the developer which files it pulled from, which it changed, and what it assumed about how the field is used.","Performance optimization: A developer asks AI to speed up a slow page. AI looks across the query, the caching layer, and the calling code to find where the time actually goes, then proposes a fix: explaining the trade-off (memory vs. speed) and what it assumed about typical data size.","Refactoring: A developer asks AI to split a large module. AI first traces everywhere the module is used across the codebase, then proposes a split that keeps those call sites working: flagging which files it touched, which it left untouched, and why."],"origins":["Coding/Programming: Facilitate multi- and cross-context coding support, flag when full scope is unavailable.","Coding/Programming: Enforce coding standards by AI as non-mandatory recommendations with proactive quality assurance and testing.","Performance Optimization: Contextualize performance optimization suggestions with relevant trade-offs, using project-specific and runtime knowledge.","Refactoring & Maintenance: Enforce project- and platform-specific coding standards during refactoring.","Refactoring & Maintenance: Limit AI to advisory mode with human review when refactoring and maintenance requires cross-system context, unwritten conventions, or undocumented decisions — and flag its own knowledge gaps."],"table_practices":["Correlate dashboards, logs, and incidents to guide engineering decisions","Trace change impacts across repositories and related applications","Maintain indexed project context that agents can draw on","Constrain agents with documented rules and responsibility boundaries","Compare design options with AI, then choose the direction yourself","Make AI state scope, assumptions, and confidence explicitly"],"practices":[{"name":"Correlate dashboards, logs, and incidents to guide engineering decisions","definition":"AI combines large amounts of operational evidence into patterns or summaries that guide priorities, investment, or diagnosis.","includes":"Synthesis across several dashboards, incident sets, logs, documents, or data sources for a decision.","excludes":"Locating one specific fault.","quotes":[{"text":"For example, use correlation of multiple dashboards to find, predict or diagnose issues that looking at individual ones would not be so clear.","who":"MS77"},{"text":"I use AI to analyze logs, review designs, explore solution alternatives, and generate implementation plans and documentation, helping me make faster and better engineering decisions.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"AI is incredibly good and fast to consume large amounts of data to provide summarized data to help us make better decisions.","who":"MS107"}]},{"name":"Trace change impacts across repositories and related applications","definition":"AI follows dependencies and related codebases to show where a proposed change must land or what else it could affect.","includes":"Cross-repository, cross-application, upstream, or partner-code impact analysis.","excludes":"Orienting within one unfamiliar codebase.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to make sense of partner teams' work when there our team has an issue with a dependency, and from there I can have AI quickly draft a fix for their team to review","who":"MS160"},{"text":"Coordinate changes across multiple codebases - many of my team's apps are closely related, so when developing it helps to think of them as a unit. AI can easily point to the impact of a change across the ecosystem.","who":"MS144"}]},{"name":"Maintain indexed project context that agents can draw on","definition":"Developers curate or use tools to index repository context, keep it current, and supply the right background for changes.","includes":"Context files, repository indexes, background scanners, or context-engineering tools.","excludes":"Durable human-readable explainers.","quotes":[{"text":"give AI a task directly and see if it can figure out itself by agents.md and other docs already there. if the design has some issue, will point out and change direction and then go to implementation phase.","who":"MS92"},{"text":"I have manually built context of the applications I work with AI, if in the future there is background AI tool that can daily scan the application and update the context as other systems change, it will be great.","who":"MS113"},{"text":"Copilot CLI /VS Code helps in in bringing the right context and helps writing intelligent code","who":"MS177"}]},{"name":"Constrain agents with documented rules and responsibility boundaries","definition":"Developers provide plans, repository documentation, criteria, or guardrails that bound what an agent should consider and do.","includes":"Persistent or task-specific instructions that limit agent scope and responsibilities.","excludes":"Reusable instructions derived from repeated work.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI on an almost daily basis as a coach to help me identify and prioritize work. I have set guardrails for my agent to prioritize work based on certain criteria as well as to assure suggestions fully fit into my realm of responsibility","who":"MS158"},{"text":"I would say that most of this here is already in use. I use LLMs to generate plans and specs for work to be done. This helps define a specific scope that agents can adhere to (but they still drift at times). I have used LLMs to review large code bases to understand potential bugs and gain an understanding more quickly of complex build systems.","who":"MS14"},{"text":"Use copilot for majority of the coding, code review. Use agent that driven by documentation for [incident system] investigation, bug fixing","who":"MS164"}]},{"name":"Compare design options with AI, then choose the direction yourself","definition":"AI widens the option set and surfaces trade-offs or drawbacks, while the developer decides which design to pursue.","includes":"Architecture discussions, alternative generation, trade-off analysis, and solution refinement before choosing.","excludes":"Turning a settled design into a reviewed implementation plan.","quotes":[{"text":"Provide me as many alternate solutions as possible to make through i have thought through all scenarios","who":"MS95"},{"text":"I use AI to analyze logs, review designs, explore solution alternatives, and generate implementation plans and documentation, helping me make faster and better engineering decisions.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"The primary use cases I have for AI in my daily work-life: (a) reviewing my code, (b) talking through design decisions and trade-offs, (c) debugging non-trivial errors, (d) performing extensive-but-straightforward refactorings that I dictate and can personally verify, (e) writing unit tests that I can review.","who":"MS30"},{"text":"I am in the process of working with a partner team to change the way it queries during automations. I am using AI to help, but it isn't quite there yet, and it does not yet understand the drawbacks of some of my ideas or how to rank the best approach. I have to have a pretty good Idea of what I want and it does not quite know that parts of it won't work.","who":"MS90"}]},{"name":"Make AI state scope, assumptions, and confidence explicitly","definition":"The developer asks AI to define its scope, expose assumptions, and state confidence, then checks those claims before proceeding.","includes":"Explicit scope control, assumption validation, or confidence reporting for design work.","excludes":"Demanding evidence for factual claims.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to diagnose unfamiliar code and automate routine changes in small, reviewable steps. I’d verify its sources and assumptions, then personally approve any security- or performance-sensitive decisions.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"They are currently in use, on things like design work that I use an AI to try to create designs I attempt to drive AI to define the scope, stay within that scope, and give confidence scores on what it perceives the feasibility of different aspects of that design are, how fleshed out they are. Where it may need more investigation.","who":"MS70"},{"text":"To validate assumptions and being able to scale out more","who":"MS21"}]},{"name":"Ask AI to combine source information to guide decisions and development","definition":"Gather documents or data and have AI analyze them to reach conclusions, choose technologies, or build an interface informed by those sources. Revisit technology choices as the source information changes.","includes":"Analyzing gathered material, combining sources for technology choices, and turning comprehensive documents into a workflow interface.","excludes":"Building trial applications for exploration or later production work.","quotes":[{"text":"Often we get to gather material and data from source systems and use LLMs to come to conclusions after analyzing them","who":"PF13"},{"text":"Synthesizing context from multiple sources for tech stack decision making with live updates so that decisions may be revised as technology evolves","who":"PF26"}]}]},{"id":"DV3","category":"DV","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to make sense of unfamiliar project context and diagnose problems; demand AI to provide traceable, source-linked explanations and recommendations.","scenarios":["Debugging: A developer asks AI why a function returns the wrong value. AI explains the cause and points to the exact lines (and the doc) it based its answer on, so the developer can confirm it isn't a hallucination by the AI.","Code Comprehension: A developer new to a service asks AI to explain how it handles user login end to end. AI walks through the flow and links each step to the specific files, instead of describing it only in general terms.","Refactoring: A developer asks AI whether a helper function is safe to delete. AI shows, with links, everywhere the helper is and isn't used, so the developer decides from evidence."],"origins":["Coding/Programming: Support codebase comprehension through traceable, source-linked explanations.","Bug Fixing/Debugging: Deploy AI as a collaborative debugging partner, with traceable, evidence-based suggestions and explanations.","Bug Fixing/Debugging: Provide multi-context-aware debugging by surfacing tacit knowledge from different sources."],"table_practices":["Navigate unfamiliar code with AI before changing it","Demand explicit sources for every AI claim","Have AI investigate incidents and locate affected code paths","Condense dense documentation into usable explanations","Create and maintain AI-written explainer documents for complex systems","Ask AI to pinpoint logic flaws and error-handling problems"],"practices":[{"name":"Navigate unfamiliar code with AI before changing it","definition":"AI explains or maps unfamiliar libraries, projects, code paths, or legacy systems so the developer can begin work faster.","includes":"Immediate orientation or question answering about an unfamiliar codebase.","excludes":"Cross-repository impact analysis.","quotes":[{"text":"I'm fairly new on my team and codebase, so when AI can help provide a map to the codebase, I will use that often.","who":"MS20"},{"text":"I’d use AI to generate boilerplate, explain unfamiliar code, suggest fixes, and create tests while I focus on design and complex logic.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"'- quickly catch up the context on a project in Q&A manner - design prototypes with heavy human in the loop - automate & iterate CI and CD for prod","who":"MS35"},{"text":"I have AI wrote code for me to review all the time. And I have often used it to explain code I a didn't write or I am not familiar with. […] No","who":"PF22"},{"text":"Use AI to understand security issue step by step, use AI to develop fix, evaluate fix, fix review. Use AI to understand unfamiliar code bases to ram up sooner.","who":"MS55"},{"text":"I use the AI to automate routine works in my dev team everyday. Also use AI to analyze logs which is much faster, take help to understand unfamiliar contexts etc.","who":"MS213"},{"text":"I use AI to make sense of partner teams' work when there our team has an issue with a dependency, and from there I can have AI quickly draft a fix for their team to review","who":"MS160"},{"text":"I use AI to summarize how unfamiliar pieces of code work for me, to better understand how new code needs to interact with old. To understand what legacy systems remain in place and why.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"research and teach me about data and performance on a program, onboard me, plan for increasing the data we log, feed data to AI, research how to optimize with additional data the program, implement","who":"PF15"},{"text":"I’d use AI to diagnose unfamiliar code and automate routine changes in small, reviewable steps. I’d verify its sources and assumptions, then personally approve any security- or performance-sensitive decisions.","who":"MS117"}]},{"name":"Demand explicit sources for every AI claim","definition":"Developers ask AI to ground conclusions in repository content or established documents so they can independently verify them.","includes":"Requests for citations, sources, code locations, or a checkable reasoning trail.","excludes":"Checking proposed scope and assumptions.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to diagnose unfamiliar code and automate routine changes in small, reviewable steps. I’d verify its sources and assumptions, then personally approve any security- or performance-sensitive decisions.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"I often need to tell AI \"show me the source behind claim X\". 75% of the time it does and that's helpful because now I know where to look in the code. 25% of the time it apologizes and tells me it had no source.","who":"MS24"},{"text":"AI is most valuable when it can work autonomously within clear boundaries while keeping its reasoning, assumptions, sources, and changes visible. Human review should focus on risk and intent rather than requiring approval for every routine step.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I want the work it does to be traceable and easy to reproduce/walk through. One thing that is hard today is remembering the why behind a choice (small/big) since being less hands-on while considering options/root-causing/etc can result in the process being less sticky in my mind.","who":"MS18"},{"text":"Using AI to understand parts of a codebase, or to review PRs, has already been really helpful. The one about source-linked explanations and recommendations sounds great, though in my experience, it still requires some amount of healthy skepticism since sometimes the conclusions/recommendations trace back to some flaw in the reasoning chain.","who":"MS8"},{"text":"The biggest value is having AI synthesize context from scattered sources (logs, configs, docs) and explain it with traceable, source-linked reasoning so I can verify it. I want it to disclose its assumptions and flag uncertainty instead of sounding confident when it's guessing — that trust boundary is what makes me comfortable adopting it for day-to-day dev work.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Intent drift and auditability are critical","who":"MS179"},{"text":"Using AI to ramp up on an unfamiliar project and ground the answers from the repo or established documents.","who":"MS162"},{"text":"I'd love to have some confirmations of the claims and solutions. Also, I'd love the idea of avoiding over-engineering or building something I didn't ask it to build.","who":"MS142"},{"text":"AI is most valuable as a copilot—not a replacement for human judgment. Recommendations should be explainable, auditable, and require approval before making production changes.","who":"MS88"}]},{"name":"Have AI investigate incidents and locate affected code paths","definition":"AI performs the first diagnostic pass over bugs, logs, crashes, or incidents and points the developer toward the cause or relevant code.","includes":"Root-cause investigation, log analysis, crash debugging, and fault localization for a specific failure.","excludes":"Pattern finding across many operational sources.","quotes":[{"text":"the skills like /review and /security-review have been very helpful in this scenario. Also the mcp servers like kusto are cherry on top for debugging purpose.","who":"MS101"},{"text":"I use the AI to automate routine works in my dev team everyday. Also use AI to analyze logs which is much faster, take help to understand unfamiliar contexts etc.","who":"MS213"},{"text":"The primary use cases I have for AI in my daily work-life: (a) reviewing my code, (b) talking through design decisions and trade-offs, (c) debugging non-trivial errors, (d) performing extensive-but-straightforward refactorings that I dictate and can personally verify, (e) writing unit tests that I can review.","who":"MS30"},{"text":"I often find myself asking AI to review unstaged changes and report its findings back to me so that I can look them over and approve any proposed changes. When investigating a bug, I will also ask AI to help me conduct an initial investigation first to guide me and help me understand the affected code paths better, especially if the bug is in an area unfamiliar to me.","who":"MS200"},{"text":"Given an [incident system] incident, ask AI to find the issue, and show me in the code where the problem resides","who":"MS134"},{"text":"Use copilot for majority of the coding, code review. Use agent that driven by documentation for [incident system] investigation, bug fixing","who":"MS164"}]},{"name":"Condense dense documentation into usable explanations","definition":"AI summarizes documentation or research, supplies assumed context, and translates difficult jargon into language the developer can use.","includes":"Understanding documents, papers, or topics rather than source code.","excludes":"Learning through a real coding task.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to read documentations or research paper about new topics. It can help answer questions I may have or give context which the paper may assume.","who":"MS135"},{"text":"I use copilot everyday for any questions and summarization and explanation of topics I need to work on my project. I also use copilot to create beautiful presentations, posters etc.","who":"MS145"},{"text":"Learning unfamiliar topic and context for unfamiliar projects","who":"MS153"}]},{"name":"Create and maintain AI-written explainer documents for complex systems","definition":"AI turns a codebase or service into durable, human-readable reference material and keeps that material aligned with later changes.","includes":"Persisted explainers, summaries, reference lists, or maintained context documents.","excludes":"One-off conversational orientation.","quotes":[{"text":"Some of this exists today, I use it regularly. It can be integrated and packaged better. One use AI is to parse large codebases and create human readable documents. Keep documents consistent with latest changes etc.","who":"MS68"},{"text":"I've used AI to build explainer documents for services and products I need to work on but are unfamiliar with. The technology is still young enough to need careful human oversight and always advise against users blindly trusting output.","who":"MS111"},{"text":"Summarizing available information for a new project and documenting for myself the useful data sources where I can find references.","who":"MS131"}]},{"name":"Ask AI to pinpoint logic flaws and error-handling problems","definition":"Use AI while fixing bugs to identify the specific logic flaws and error handling problems behind them.","includes":"Diagnosing bugs by locating faulty logic or gaps in handling errors.","excludes":"Assessing the consequences of proposed changes.","quotes":[{"text":"It really helps in bug fixing especially pointing the exact logic flaws and error handling […] It can be used to do a self-review before a peer review during development phase or during pre-deployment to plan for affected items or downstream applications","who":"PF21"}]}]},{"id":"DV4","category":"DV","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to create changes in human-reviewable increments; demand AI to hold consequential changes for human sign-off before applying them.","scenarios":["Refactoring: A developer asks AI to migrate a shared date-parsing utility that forty modules import to a new library. A silent behavior change would corrupt data everywhere it runs. AI migrates one caller, shows the diff, and waits for approval before touching the next, so drift gets caught before it spreads.","Bug fixing: A developer asks AI to apply a fix across five services that deserialize a shared event schema. Rolling it out everywhere at once risks a version mismatch that breaks the pipeline. AI proposes the change for the first service, waits for a yes, then moves to the next.","Performance: A developer asks AI to add caching to a hot module. Wrong invalidation would serve stale data to users and pass every test. Instead of shipping it all at once, AI submits the read path as a small reviewable change and holds the invalidation logic for explicit sign-off before it goes live."],"origins":["Bug Fixing/Debugging: Require human authorization before applying any incident mitigation or remediation.","Quality & Risk, Testing & Quality Assurance: Enforce proactive quality assurance and testing alongside every performance optimization suggestion, enabling developers to validate and approve.","Refactoring & Maintenance: Propagate multi-file refactoring changes with an explicit impact map, with human review.","Refactoring & Maintenance: Require explicit human approval for structural or bulk refactoring changes.","AI Development/Integration: Prohibit autonomous AI deployment to production AI pipelines without human approval and accountability.","AI Development/Integration: Require mandatory human review before AI-generated code enters AI pipelines."],"table_practices":["Break large changes into small increments and verify each one","Let AI diagnose and propose changes in small, reviewable steps, and verify its sources and assumptions","Review generated code continuously and redirect the agent during implementation","Let AI reach a draft pull request before intervening","Run an independent review agent before humans inspect the pull request","Block consequential actions until a human explicitly approves them"],"practices":[{"name":"Break large changes into small increments and verify each one","definition":"The developer asks AI to deliver separately reviewable steps, diffs, or subtasks and checks each before continuing.","includes":"Increment-by-increment implementation paired with human verification.","excludes":"A single delayed gate.","quotes":[{"text":"Collaboratively developing a strategy/infrastructure for a given project with AI. Then using AI to produce content, but for one section of this strategy at a time with back and forth testing, validation and iteration of AI outputs","who":"PF23"},{"text":"I often work using AI to generate code to solve a problem based on a large design. I instruct the AI to break up these changes into incremental changes that can be reviewed by the team so we can parse what is actually being changed.","who":"MS47"},{"text":"I would use AI to investigate unfamiliar code, trace relevant dependencies, and propose a small implementation plan before making changes. I would then have it implement the work in reviewable increments, with targeted tests and explicit human approval for security-sensitive, performance-critical, or broadly consequential changes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like generating test cases from a spec, parsing regression logs, and drafting ADO work items — but I keep it in human-reviewable increments: it proposes a plan and reviewable diffs, and I sign off before anything is applied or pushed. For anything security- or performance-critical, I make the final call myself rather than letting the AI finalize it.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Working with AI to break the whole assignment into sub tasks, review together those tasks, approve\\require changes, ask to implement, make local review together and approve\\ask for changes, create a PR, using separate dedicated agent to run deep review while the dev agent to monitor for PR comment, review together, address and comment back. Monitor for the PR to be merged, deployed in to Test and trigger verification steps and providing final report. Iterate based on the needs.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"I heavily use Copilot for development work, but I break it down into small pieces and verify each step. I use my experience to know when something sounds really off, and I investigate further.","who":"MS10"},{"text":"I’d use AI to diagnose unfamiliar code and automate routine changes in small, reviewable steps. I’d verify its sources and assumptions, then personally approve any security- or performance-sensitive decisions.","who":"MS117"}]},{"name":"Review generated code continuously and redirect the agent during implementation","definition":"The developer inspects emerging code, gives feedback, and edits or redirects the agent while the work is still being produced.","includes":"Live local review, pair programming, or hand-editing agent suggestions into source.","excludes":"Reviewing a completed diff.","quotes":[{"text":"I would, and currently do, use all of the above in my development work. I do a lot of 'pair programming' with an agent, where we will discuss the changes I want to make and either it provides the code that I then review and edit into the source files (or ask questions about), or I make the changes and it reviews them and points out any issues it sees in my code.","who":"MS154"},{"text":"Working with AI to break the whole assignment into sub tasks, review together those tasks, approve\\require changes, ask to implement, make local review together and approve\\ask for changes, create a PR, using separate dedicated agent to run deep review while the dev agent to monitor for PR comment, review together, address and comment back. Monitor for the PR to be merged, deployed in to Test and trigger verification steps and providing final report. Iterate based on the needs.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"I already do lots of local code review as the agent is writing code and provide feedback, so the tasks above are common practice for me.","who":"MS169"}]},{"name":"Let AI reach a draft pull request before intervening","definition":"AI investigates, implements, builds, tests, and prepares a draft pull request without intermediate approval; active human review starts there.","includes":"The first meaningful human gate is at draft pull request or repository check-in.","excludes":"Approval after every increment.","quotes":[{"text":"I usually use AI to understand the issue and create a draft PR with the fix. If there is any scope for routine & repeat work, I usually create a skill and check it into the repo.","who":"MS23"}]},{"name":"Run an independent review agent before humans inspect the pull request","definition":"A separate agent or second model checks generated code for correctness, security, architecture, and practices before team review.","includes":"An automated review layer distinct from the producing agent and placed before people review.","excludes":"AI assisting the same developer's review.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to drive my development process from ticket to PR. I have it do automated reviews with second models on PRs created, before my team has an opportunity to review.","who":"MS126"},{"text":"Working with AI to break the whole assignment into sub tasks, review together those tasks, approve\\require changes, ask to implement, make local review together and approve\\ask for changes, create a PR, using separate dedicated agent to run deep review while the dev agent to monitor for PR comment, review together, address and comment back. Monitor for the PR to be merged, deployed in to Test and trigger verification steps and providing final report. Iterate based on the needs.","who":"MS85"}]},{"name":"Block consequential actions until a human explicitly approves them","definition":"AI may prepare changes, but a human-controlled stop prevents application, commit, push, or continuation until approval.","includes":"Explicit gates or mechanisms that halt consequential actions pending human input.","excludes":"A single checkpoint at the draft pull request.","quotes":[{"text":"AI can improve development speed, but generated code still needs human review for correctness, security, and maintainability.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"I have AI wrote code for me to review all the time. And I have often used it to explain code I a didn't write or I am not familiar with. […] No","who":"PF22"},{"text":"I don't trust AI to submit any code; I'd rather that AI provide comments or static artifacts such as automated tooling and scripts that can be verified before they are run.","who":"MS26"},{"text":"I feel like we do most those things already in some capacity by having it create plans in markdown for review or in chat directly. Code is then AI and human reviewed to ensure quality.","who":"MS39"},{"text":"I would use AI to support debugging and development tasks while keeping important changes under human review. […] AI can make development more efficient and help with learning, but it is still important to understand and review its suggestions","who":"PF40"},{"text":"I would use AI to investigate unfamiliar code, trace relevant dependencies, and propose a small implementation plan before making changes. I would then have it implement the work in reviewable increments, with targeted tests and explicit human approval for security-sensitive, performance-critical, or broadly consequential changes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like generating test cases from a spec, parsing regression logs, and drafting ADO work items — but I keep it in human-reviewable increments: it proposes a plan and reviewable diffs, and I sign off before anything is applied or pushed. For anything security- or performance-critical, I make the final call myself rather than letting the AI finalize it.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Automate repetitive tasks, review and finalize completed units.","who":"PF8"},{"text":"In my experience, it it too soon for any sort of fully automated development to be a good idea.","who":"MS169"},{"text":"The only thing I don't trust AI to do is to approve PRs - every line of code should be reviewed by a human.","who":"MS80"}]},{"name":"Have AI explain changes across repositories so people can trace and review them","definition":"Use AI to author changes across several repositories and provide accompanying output that people can trace and review.","includes":"Changes spanning repositories with traceable, reviewable accompanying output.","excludes":"General human review of generated code.","quotes":[{"text":"Author complex PRs spanning multiple repositories. generating traceable and reviewable output along with changes.","who":"PF18"}]}]},{"id":"DV5","category":"DV","kind":"use","text":"Use AI as a coach for skill building; demand AI to provide clear explanations and uncertainty indicators.","scenarios":["Learning to code: A junior developer asks AI how to structure a class. Instead of writing it for them, AI walks through the options and the reasoning so the developer learns to do it themselves.","Coding: A developer asks AI to review their approach. AI explains why one pattern is riskier and flags the parts it isn't confident about, so the developer double-checks those.","Debugging: A developer asks AI to help them find a bug (not fix it). AI asks guiding questions and points to where to look, keeping the developer in the driver's seat."],"origins":["Coding/Programming: Preserve learning, skill-building, and craftsmanship."],"table_practices":["Learn by solving real tasks with AI","Talk through ideas with AI as a pair-programming partner","Use AI coaching to prepare junior developers"],"practices":[{"name":"Learn by solving real tasks with AI","definition":"The developer uses AI as a coach while completing real work in a new project, topic, or language and learns from the output and its mistakes.","includes":"Skill building tied to doing an actual task in unfamiliar territory.","excludes":"Immediate code orientation without a learning aim.","quotes":[{"text":"Using AI to coach and skill build would be helpful in understanding new projects or programming languages as each developer has the potential to contribute to more projects with assistance from AI agents.","who":"MS192"},{"text":"If I am working on the unknown new areas for the first time, I would like AI to help me with the issue that I am working on along with that improve my knowledge of the new area. It would be like coach and assistant.","who":"MS62"}]},{"name":"Talk through ideas with AI as a pair-programming partner","definition":"The developer sharpens reasoning through dialogue with AI, alternating among questions, proposed code, review, and refinement.","includes":"Conversational idea testing or rubber-duck-style pair work used to improve the developer's thinking.","excludes":"Live oversight primarily controlling generated code.","quotes":[{"text":"I use like a companion in my work, I talk to it and provide it with context, and we both work together to refine the solution eventually to a point where things are good. We also ideate on ways to speed up this process if possible.","who":"MS198"},{"text":"I would, and currently do, use all of the above in my development work. I do a lot of 'pair programming' with an agent, where we will discuss the changes I want to make and either it provides the code that I then review and edit into the source files (or ask questions about), or I make the changes and it reviews them and points out any issues it sees in my code.","who":"MS154"}]},{"name":"Use AI coaching to prepare junior developers","definition":"AI supports skill growth for junior developers or people preparing for greater responsibility, often without a formal coaching program.","includes":"Coaching aimed at career development, promotion readiness, or future technical leadership.","excludes":"Self-directed learning during a task.","quotes":[{"text":"… It should also be used as a tool for learning especially for junior developers so that when time comes for them to lead, they are much more knowledgeable of best software practices.","who":"MS155"}]}]},{"id":"DV6","category":"DV","kind":"limit","text":"Prohibit AI from making or finalizing security- and performance-critical development decisions.","scenarios":["Security: A developer can have AI review authentication code and suggest fixes, but a human writes and signs off on the final security-critical logic.","Performance: AI may propose optimizations for a critical path, but the developer makes the final call on what actually ships.","Architecture: For a decision with long-term security impact, AI advises only; the human decides."],"origins":["Coding/Programming: Exclude AI from authoring security- and privacy-critical code.","Coding/Programming: Reserve final architectural and design decisions for the developer.","Performance Optimization: Restrict AI from autonomous performance optimizations without human review.","Performance Optimization: Preserve engineering ownership optimization tasks for accountability and craftsmanship."],"table_practices":["Reserve final security- and performance-critical decisions for human sign-off, and approve sensitive changes personally"],"practices":[{"name":"Reserve final security- and performance-critical decisions for human sign-off, and approve sensitive changes personally","definition":"AI may assess or recommend security- or performance-critical choices, but a stricter human checkpoint makes the final call before such changes proceed.","includes":"Personal sign-off on critical decisions, or a dedicated approval gate triggered by security or performance risk.","excludes":"Using AI to find or fix security and performance defects.","quotes":[{"text":"I would use AI to investigate unfamiliar code, trace relevant dependencies, and propose a small implementation plan before making changes. I would then have it implement the work in reviewable increments, with targeted tests and explicit human approval for security-sensitive, performance-critical, or broadly consequential changes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like generating test cases from a spec, parsing regression logs, and drafting ADO work items — but I keep it in human-reviewable increments: it proposes a plan and reviewable diffs, and I sign off before anything is applied or pushed. For anything security- or performance-critical, I make the final call myself rather than letting the AI finalize it.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"I’d use AI to diagnose unfamiliar code and automate routine changes in small, reviewable steps. I’d verify its sources and assumptions, then personally approve any security- or performance-sensitive decisions.","who":"MS117"}]},{"name":"Use AI to find and fix security or performance defects","definition":"AI helps find vulnerabilities, understand critical issues, develop and evaluate fixes, or optimize performance-sensitive code.","includes":"Hands-on AI assistance with security findings, security fixes, or performance optimization.","excludes":"Independent pre-submission auditing.","quotes":[{"text":"the skills like /review and /security-review have been very helpful in this scenario. Also the mcp servers like kusto are cherry on top for debugging purpose.","who":"MS101"},{"text":"Use AI to understand security issue step by step, use AI to develop fix, evaluate fix, fix review. Use AI to understand unfamiliar code bases to ram up sooner.","who":"MS55"},{"text":"I already use AI in many of the ways described. I've even used it in security work to help find vulnerabilities and performance work to help optimize blocks of code.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"- Refactoring the large module - Bug fixes - performance improvement","who":"MS214"}]}]},{"id":"DP1","category":"DP","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to find and synthesize information from different sources with clear source attribution; demand AI to surface historical context and inconsistencies for human review.","scenarios":["Requirements: A developer asks AI to pull the requirements out of a month of emails and Teams chats. AI produces a summary with links back to each source and flags where two messages contradict each other for a human to resolve.","Architecture: A developer asks AI to summarize past design decisions for a service. AI gathers them, cites where each came from, and notes an older decision that seems to clash with the current plan.","Planning: A developer asks AI to consolidate feature requests from several docs. AI lists them with sources and points out duplicates and inconsistencies instead of silently merging them."],"origins":["Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Aggregate requirements from heterogeneous sources with source attributions; validate completeness and flag gaps iteratively during requirements gathering and analysis.","System Architecture & Design: Generate system overviews and diagrams from existing code and artifacts.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Synthesize planning-relevant history from cross-platform communications.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Surface cross-team dependencies and blockers as human-actionable flags."],"table_practices":["Consolidate internal project sources before planning or designing","Research prior solutions and other teams' practices before designing","Demand that AI link its findings to specific sources so humans can verify them","Have AI summarize requirements after client meetings"],"practices":[{"name":"Consolidate internal project sources before planning or designing","definition":"AI collects specifications, logs, discussions, wikis, transcripts, and other internal material into one view that supports planning or design.","includes":"Collecting, summarizing, or consolidating several existing internal project sources.","excludes":"Researching precedents outside the immediate project.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to collect requirements from diverse sources, discuss limitations, and create documents for stakeholder review, on a daily basis.","who":"MS126"},{"text":"AI help gather sources and give information about components and how they work together. Then propose several options for the implementations. I will choose one","who":"MS92"},{"text":"Synthesizing conversations, decisions, and discussions from disparate sources via AI is extremely helpful. I use AI the most when doing the work ABOUT the work like documentation..","who":"MS111"},{"text":"Asking the AI to run deep research, find relevant documentation across all available knowledge bases, explore existing solutions\\designed for similar problem\\work. come up with proper design which fully explain the reason for the suggested design, trade offs.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"I use my agent to process large amounts of existing content (such as multiple wiki pages) and provide me with a consolidated view on topics I'm researching. I also use it to help keep track of project statuses and next steps, and to surface reminders as needed to keep things running smoothly","who":"MS154"},{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"In design and planning I use AI to find and synthesize context from multiple sources — prior test plans, specs, and past regression results — to shape an approach and surface expected trade-offs before I commit to it. I demand it disclose its scope and assumptions and hold the consequential decisions for my sign-off, so the AI accelerates the exploration but I own the final plan.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"I use AI to explore design and architecture alternatives, analyze trade-offs, and quickly synthesize information from logs, specifications, documentation, and historical discussions before making decisions. I also use AI to generate downstream artifacts such as design documents, status updates, ADO comments, presentations, and implementation plans, while ensuring the outputs remain traceable and are reviewed by humans before any significant design or project decisions are finalized.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"gather context and present it for me so I can plan and create my mental models","who":"MS35"},{"text":"I use AI to learn different systems connected and business process involved to design better.","who":"MS145"}]},{"name":"Research prior solutions and other teams' practices before designing","definition":"AI searches earlier designs, code, other teams, open-source projects, and comparable solutions to ground a new design.","includes":"Looking for reusable precedents or historical context in prior or external work.","excludes":"Combining the current project's internal records.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI for most design work now. As a subject matter expert, I can utilize AI to quickly iterate over historical project norms, find faults in designs, iterate quickly, and find gaps in designs that normally would take me a lot longer manually.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"Asking the AI to run deep research, find relevant documentation across all available knowledge bases, explore existing solutions\\designed for similar problem\\work. come up with proper design which fully explain the reason for the suggested design, trade offs.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I often will have AI look at practices followed by other teams/major open-source companies to ground decision making. Manually looking into multiple unknown codebases is much slower for me to work through. Having it link back to specifics (sometimes code, sometimes historical design docs) would help build confidence.","who":"MS18"},{"text":"I use AI to research information on technology trade-offs to inform design","who":"MS109"}]},{"name":"Demand that AI link its findings to specific sources so humans can verify them","definition":"AI attaches findings to concrete code, documents, or source material so people can check the evidence before trusting it.","includes":"Explicit requests for citations, links, source references, or verifiable context on findings.","excludes":"Links between approved designs and derived artifacts.","quotes":[{"text":"Always probe AI responses further during conversation, demanding sources for outputs. Playing Devil's Advocate to ensure that AI strategies are well-reasoned before beginning to implement them","who":"PF23"},{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I often will have AI look at practices followed by other teams/major open-source companies to ground decision making. Manually looking into multiple unknown codebases is much slower for me to work through. Having it link back to specifics (sometimes code, sometimes historical design docs) would help build confidence.","who":"MS18"},{"text":"AI should both be limited from making final decisions but also used to create verifiable context for humans to make those decisions.","who":"MS130"}]},{"name":"Have AI summarize requirements after client meetings","definition":"Use AI to summarize requirements discussed in meetings with clients.","includes":"Requirements summaries based on client meetings.","excludes":"Documentation and onboarding work.","quotes":[{"text":"Summarizing the requirements after meeting with clients","who":"PF26"}]}]},{"id":"DP2","category":"DP","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to explore contextually grounded design and architecture alternatives with expected trade-off and impact analysis; demand AI to maintain provenance of design decisions.","scenarios":["Architecture: A developer asks AI for a few ways to structure a new service. AI proposes options grounded in the existing system, lays out the trade-offs, and records why each was suggested.","Requirements/Design: A developer asks AI to explore database options for a use case. AI compares them on cost and speed and keeps a trail of which requirement drove each recommendation.","Design: A developer asks AI what happens if they tighten a recovery-time target. AI shows the downstream impact and links it back to the earlier decisions it depends on."],"origins":["Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Explore design, technology, and architecture options with structured trade-off analysis.","System Architecture & Design: Provide contextualized architecture ideation and design exploration support with trade-offs and risk assessment.","System Architecture & Design: Maintain a traceable, queryable log of design decisions and their downstream impacts."],"table_practices":["Brainstorm architecture ideas with AI before development starts","Generate several design options with trade-offs, then choose among them","Have AI draft the design, then refine it with expert review","Have AI critique a draft design for gaps, unstated assumptions, and corner cases"],"practices":[{"name":"Brainstorm architecture ideas with AI before development starts","definition":"AI serves as an early thinking partner for discussion, ideation, and fast prototyping, while the developer incorporates selected ideas into their design.","includes":"Conversational brainstorming, rubber-ducking, or prototyping before implementation.","excludes":"Having AI author the design.","quotes":[{"text":"I currently use AI in planning, especially while ideating over concepts that we may want to apply.","who":"MS52"},{"text":"I think it's a good brainstormer for architecture and design but it should never be used to make decisions.","who":"MS129"},{"text":"AI can't make 100% correct design yet, but it still can come up with good ideas that I would incorporate into my own designs","who":"MS207"},{"text":"I regularly use AI for assistance with architectural topics (ask), typing things for me (agent), etc. However, I do not want it performing any actions autonomously without me instructing it to do so.","who":"MS127"},{"text":"I marked more no's in this section because I think much of the stuff here is about working with other people and thinking hard about things. AI is super helpful during design for things like fast prototyping of options, but having it drive the thinking or collaboration will IMO lead to worse outcomes and less creative output.","who":"MS8"},{"text":"Help me come up with design idea ans structure document","who":"MS87"},{"text":"Discussing ideas and potential architecture approaches before starting development.","who":"MS21"}]},{"name":"Generate several design options with trade-offs, then choose among them","definition":"AI produces multiple plausible architecture or implementation candidates and lays out their trade-offs, risks, dependencies, and likely impacts; the person makes the choice.","includes":"Requests for alternatives, comparisons, counterfactuals, pros and cons, or impact analysis where the human keeps the selection.","excludes":"Open-ended early ideation without structured options; critique of a draft.","quotes":[{"text":"AI has been particularly helpful in troubleshooting issues and evaluating logs. It has also been a great \"sounding board\" for me to bounce ideas off of before committing to any given direction.","who":"MS158"},{"text":"When designing a new feature, i use AI to collate information about that feature and different approaches and trade-offs. I then review it and make a decision based on my specific feature requirements.","who":"MS103"},{"text":"I’d use AI to quickly explore design options, summarize research, and turn rough ideas into a structured plan. I’d still review the output and make the final decisions based on user needs and project constraints.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"In early stages of feature work needing investigation into architectural options and the most fitting implementation, using AI to explore various design candidates and return an analysis of the tradeoffs of each can be very helpful.","who":"MS200"},{"text":"for some of these things (ex. performance, sec decisions), it is good to brainstorm with AI and figure out the options with various assumptions and let the human make the final decisions abased on the criteria. AI can make suggestions.","who":"MS110"},{"text":"Just having to describe how something should work to an AI is an upgrade to the rubber-duck process. The conversational element works much better to get me started working out my thoughts than writing my own notes without anyone to bounce ideas off of.","who":"MS27"},{"text":"It gives speed for understading multiple design option for same solution. So that final approach could be decided. […] Yes, Develpor should move towards building small ML models to meed the solution which requires internal workflow embeded in these tiny models","who":"PF17"},{"text":"The items prohibiting and limiting the use of LLMs seem the most useful and impactful. Using LLMs to \"convert reviewed designs into downstream artifacts\" and \"to explore design and architecture alternatives\" is already heavily used and can be genuinely useful in some cases.","who":"MS211"},{"text":"Use AI to explore contextually grounded design and architecture alternatives with expected trade-off and impact analysis -- this is great value; AI to provide counterfactuals and exhaustive analysis on trade-off, based on larger knowledge corpus. Good designs can still be challenged.","who":"MS125"},{"text":"Use ai to enhance the documentation, look for better alternative approach in workflow and increase efficiency of the processes involved. […] Other than documentation it can be used to help onboarding resources to get a proper kt about the architecture of the system they will be working","who":"PF21"}]},{"name":"Have AI draft the design, then refine it with expert review","definition":"AI produces an initial architecture or design, potentially from research through documentation, and a knowledgeable person reviews and reshapes it.","includes":"AI authoring a preliminary or end-to-end design for later human refinement.","excludes":"Human-authored designs reviewed by AI.","quotes":[{"text":"I've used AI design complex systems and reviewed their architecture. I've also used to generate bugs and work-items to varying degrees of success.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"Asking the AI to run deep research, find relevant documentation across all available knowledge bases, explore existing solutions\\designed for similar problem\\work. come up with proper design which fully explain the reason for the suggested design, trade offs.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"I am leveraging AI to architect the solution from research to documenattion.","who":"MS97"}]},{"name":"Have AI critique a draft design for gaps, unstated assumptions, and corner cases","definition":"AI examines an early or draft design for missing requirements, unstated assumptions, inconsistencies, overlooked cases, and feasible better approaches, while the person decides what to change.","includes":"Design critique, pushback, gap and assumption finding on a draft or early design.","excludes":"Generating alternative designs from scratch.","quotes":[{"text":"I would want AI to find unstated assumptions in early designs. Having clear requirements and constraints is key to engineering success.","who":"MS20"},{"text":"I use AI for most design work now. As a subject matter expert, I can utilize AI to quickly iterate over historical project norms, find faults in designs, iterate quickly, and find gaps in designs that normally would take me a lot longer manually.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"Use AI to explore contextually grounded design and architecture alternatives with expected trade-off and impact analysis -- this is great value; AI to provide counterfactuals and exhaustive analysis on trade-off, based on larger knowledge corpus. Good designs can still be challenged.","who":"MS125"},{"text":"Again much of this is already trying to be orchestrated as part of designs in day to day work. Trying to get AIs to push back where it makes sense to and consider alternative sources where it makes sense to. There is a bit of a problem in that sometimes the answers are a lot more nuanced that require taking context sometimes from some sources for some implementations then idsregarding that same context in other areas.","who":"MS70"},{"text":"I will use AI to evaluate my design on its clarity and effectiveness.","who":"MS84"},{"text":"check to see if we missed any corner cases or have any better design approaches feasible.","who":"MS81"}]}]},{"id":"DP3","category":"DP","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to convert reviewed designs into downstream artifacts; demand AI to make every artifact traceable to its reviewed sources.","scenarios":["Planning: After the team approves a spec, a developer asks AI to turn it into user stories and tasks. AI generates them, each linked back to the part of the spec it came from.","Design to work: A developer asks AI to break an approved architecture doc into a task hierarchy. AI creates the tickets and tags each with its source section.","Requirements to work: A developer asks AI to generate test cases from vetted requirements. AI produces them, each referencing the requirement it covers."],"origins":["Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Generate downstream artifacts only after requirements are human-reviewed.","Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Require human review of AI-generated requirement artifacts.","System Architecture & Design: Convert finalized designs into actionable work items and starter artifacts."],"table_practices":["Generate work items and implementation plans from approved designs","Draft review-ready specifications and design documents from requirements","Approve designs before AI converts them into downstream artifacts","Keep generated artifacts linked to source designs across teams and time"],"practices":[{"name":"Generate work items and implementation plans from approved designs","definition":"AI translates designs or requirements into actionable stories, tasks, bugs, architecture drafts, and implementation steps.","includes":"Deriving concrete implementation work from substantive design material.","excludes":"Routine board administration.","quotes":[{"text":"I already use AI to help me manage my workitems. Being able to generate workitems reliably from design docs would be great as would any help it can give prioritizing them.","who":"MS3"},{"text":"The items prohibiting and limiting the use of LLMs seem the most useful and impactful. Using LLMs to \"convert reviewed designs into downstream artifacts\" and \"to explore design and architecture alternatives\" is already heavily used and can be genuinely useful in some cases.","who":"MS211"},{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I use AI to explore design and architecture alternatives, analyze trade-offs, and quickly synthesize information from logs, specifications, documentation, and historical discussions before making decisions. I also use AI to generate downstream artifacts such as design documents, status updates, ADO comments, presentations, and implementation plans, while ensuring the outputs remain traceable and are reviewed by humans before any significant design or project decisions are finalized.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"the translation layer between strategy and design to actual tasks for implementation can be beneficial.","who":"MS142"},{"text":"I’d use AI to compare design options, identify risks and dependencies, and turn requirements into architecture drafts and implementation plans.","who":"MS88"}]},{"name":"Draft review-ready specifications and design documents from requirements","definition":"AI turns requirements, discussions, transcripts, or design thinking into specifications, structured design documents, presentations, or stakeholder-review drafts.","includes":"Producing written design artifacts for subsequent review.","excludes":"Producing tasks and implementation steps.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to collect requirements from diverse sources, discuss limitations, and create documents for stakeholder review, on a daily basis.","who":"MS126"},{"text":"I use AI to explore design and architecture alternatives, analyze trade-offs, and quickly synthesize information from logs, specifications, documentation, and historical discussions before making decisions. I also use AI to generate downstream artifacts such as design documents, status updates, ADO comments, presentations, and implementation plans, while ensuring the outputs remain traceable and are reviewed by humans before any significant design or project decisions are finalized.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"Help me come up with design idea ans structure document","who":"MS87"}]},{"name":"Approve designs before AI converts them into downstream artifacts","definition":"A person reviews and approves the design or task breakdown before AI generates stories, tasks, or implementation plans.","includes":"An explicit human approval step before design-to-artifact conversion.","excludes":"Conversion without a foregrounded approval gate.","quotes":[{"text":"I'm trying to get into a flow of using AI to generate requirements, human review; generate engineering designs, human review; generate implementation and tests, human review.","who":"MS20"},{"text":"I feel like we do most those things already in some capacity by having it create plans in markdown for review or in chat directly. Code is then AI and human reviewed to ensure quality.","who":"MS39"},{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"i truly wish the build plan phases are better distributed. there should be a command or something that can be flagged that will allow one to programaticaly allow a planner model and builder seperate model. we can do it now, but i have only found a way to do it via logic commands. its important because the gate you can create ebtween the phases gives you a higher confidence level when scopping and coding.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"I quite like the \"Plan\" mode in agentic AI coding frameworks.","who":"MS120"}]},{"name":"Keep generated artifacts linked to source designs across teams and time","definition":"AI preserves traceability between reviewed designs and derived artifacts so later teams and hires can recover the context and reasoning.","includes":"Maintaining durable links from downstream artifacts to their source designs.","excludes":"Citations for research findings.","quotes":[{"text":"I would use AI to gather requirements and historical context, then generate several architecture options with explicit trade-offs, assumptions, and links to the source material. I would review and approve the decision before using AI to turn the selected design into traceable work items and implementation plans.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I use AI to explore design and architecture alternatives, analyze trade-offs, and quickly synthesize information from logs, specifications, documentation, and historical discussions before making decisions. I also use AI to generate downstream artifacts such as design documents, status updates, ADO comments, presentations, and implementation plans, while ensuring the outputs remain traceable and are reviewed by humans before any significant design or project decisions are finalized.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"Having the AI convert designs into traceable artifacts for long term design planning and evolution is a great way to keep context across teams and also across hires.","who":"MS155"}]}]},{"id":"DP4","category":"DP","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to inform task plans, schedules, and resource assignments fairly to fit the project's context and people's interests and skills; demand AI to disclose its assumptions and uncertainty explicitly.","scenarios":["Planning: A developer asks AI to draft a project schedule from a design doc. AI proposes timelines that fit the project's context, and states what it assumed about team size and availability.","Scheduling: A developer asks AI to order a set of tasks to finish fastest. AI suggests a sequence that plays to each person's skills, and flags which dependencies it is unsure about.","Resource planning: A developer asks AI to help assign tasks. AI proposes an allocation that balances load fairly against people's interests and workloads."],"origins":["Task & Project Planning / Management: Provide context-aware planning, prioritization, and scheduling proposals with uncertainty signals.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Surface cross-team dependencies and blockers as human-actionable flags."],"table_practices":["Set constraints, let AI draft the plan, then review and edit it","Run project management with AI across the full delivery cycle","Gate implementation on plan confidence or human review"],"practices":[{"name":"Set constraints, let AI draft the plan, then review and edit it","definition":"A person supplies the scope, constraints, headcount, and context; AI structures the roadmap or task plan; the person verifies and revises it.","includes":"Human-constrained plan drafting followed by human review and editing.","excludes":"Delegating the whole planning cycle.","quotes":[{"text":"I have used Copilot to help plan projects. It helps organize thoughts, but I keep close control and constantly guide it.","who":"MS10"},{"text":"Asking AI to analyze current systems and prepare a detailed task/plan that I can review before proceeding to implementation, is a workflow that has been helpful.","who":"MS212"},{"text":"I’d use AI to quickly explore design options, summarize research, and turn rough ideas into a structured plan. I’d still review the output and make the final decisions based on user needs and project constraints.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"AI provides a pretty good starting point, then AI + Human collaboration refines the plan before adopting it.","who":"MS107"}]},{"name":"Run project management with AI across the full delivery cycle","definition":"AI participates across planning, scheduling, execution, plan updates, and validation as an ongoing project manager.","includes":"Broad or end-to-end AI involvement across multiple project-management stages.","excludes":"Drafting one human-owned plan.","quotes":[{"text":"I would ask AI to help me directly manage a project once we've gone from strategic vision to an actual decision on what to implement.","who":"MS22"},{"text":"I use AI in the following way 1. Plan 2. Schedule 3. Exectute 4. Update plans 5. Validate At times I need to prompt AI to too much to get it going, specifically as it enters new context. I would want that friction to be reduced.","who":"MS113"},{"text":"an \"AI project manager\" involved in all aspects of the project","who":"MS64"}]},{"name":"Gate implementation on plan confidence or human review","definition":"The design-to-build transition pauses until the plan reaches a stated confidence threshold or passes a human readiness review.","includes":"A confidence score or explicit review gate before implementation begins.","excludes":"Checks on automated board changes.","quotes":[{"text":"Asking AI to analyze current systems and prepare a detailed task/plan that I can review before proceeding to implementation, is a workflow that has been helpful.","who":"MS212"},{"text":"Prohibit AI from making or finalizing consequential design and planning decisions: Often I have AI ask me multiple questions on a task before it starts implementation, just to make sure it confirms assumptions with me. Use AI to explore contextually grounded design and architecture: I use a custom skill to help with these kinds of tasks as well.","who":"MS216"}]}]},{"id":"DP5","category":"DP","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to automate routine work-item creation and updates (e.g., status reports); demand AI to keep every change easy to review and undo.","scenarios":["Planning: A developer asks AI to split a goal into sub-tasks in the tracker. AI creates them in a way that's easy to follow, tweak, or revert.","Status reporting: A developer asks AI to generate a weekly status report from their tickets. AI drafts it for proofreading rather than posting it.","PM upkeep: A developer asks AI to update ticket statuses after standup. AI makes the changes so they're easy to inspect and roll back if something's off."],"origins":["Task & Project Planning / Management: Automate routine work-item creation and updates.","System Architecture & Design: Convert finalized designs into actionable work items and starter artifacts.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Require human authorization before AI executes planning operations."],"table_practices":["Automate work items, status updates, and board bookkeeping","Track status, next steps, blockers, reminders, and rollout gates"],"practices":[{"name":"Automate work items, status updates, and board bookkeeping","definition":"AI creates or maintains stories, tasks, bugs, Azure DevOps entries, and related board records after substantive design work exists.","includes":"Routine generation or maintenance of work-item records and board entries.","excludes":"Choosing the substantive breakdown.","quotes":[{"text":"I've used AI design complex systems and reviewed their architecture. I've also used to generate bugs and work-items to varying degrees of success.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"I already use AI to help me manage my workitems. Being able to generate workitems reliably from design docs would be great as would any help it can give prioritizing them.","who":"MS3"},{"text":"Synthesizing conversations, decisions, and discussions from disparate sources via AI is extremely helpful. I use AI the most when doing the work ABOUT the work like documentation..","who":"MS111"},{"text":"I use AI to explore design and architecture alternatives, analyze trade-offs, and quickly synthesize information from logs, specifications, documentation, and historical discussions before making decisions. I also use AI to generate downstream artifacts such as design documents, status updates, ADO comments, presentations, and implementation plans, while ensuring the outputs remain traceable and are reviewed by humans before any significant design or project decisions are finalized.","who":"MS151"},{"text":"It is fantastic for bookkeeping, ADO management, etc as long as proper checks are in place","who":"MS179"},{"text":"i already made a work item skill that is shared across the team to provide better visibility, usage has been hard to create though","who":"MS25"}]},{"name":"Track status, next steps, blockers, reminders, and rollout gates","definition":"AI keeps a running view of execution, answers status queries, surfaces blockers and reminders, and checks rollout readiness.","includes":"Monitoring project state and alerting people when work is blocked or ready to advance.","excludes":"Writing administrative updates.","quotes":[{"text":"On top of it, create a loop to handle all the subtasks so it auto monitors and let me know the blockers. Also add gating and rollout status so it checks the rollout before the next phase of PR is sent for review","who":"MS175"},{"text":"I use my agent to process large amounts of existing content (such as multiple wiki pages) and provide me with a consolidated view on topics I'm researching. I also use it to help keep track of project statuses and next steps, and to surface reminders as needed to keep things running smoothly","who":"MS154"},{"text":"I regularly query about the status of particular efforts and where to locate valuable documentation which I can refer to.","who":"MS158"}]}]},{"id":"DP6","category":"DP","kind":"limit","text":"Prohibit AI from making or finalizing consequential design and planning decisions, such as inferring unstated requirements, deciding on architecture or business logic, or automatically assigning tasks.","scenarios":["Requirements: AI can point out gaps and ambiguities, but it must not fill in unstated requirements on its own; the human decides what's actually needed.","Architecture: AI can lay out options, but a person makes the final architectural call.","Planning: AI can suggest who might take a task, but it doesn't auto-assign work; a human confirms."],"origins":["Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Restrict AI from originating or inferring unstated requirements.","System Architecture & Design: Prohibit AI from making or finalizing architectural decisions before design is approved.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Gate team/project's strategic, assignment, and ambiguous decisions on human approval.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Require human authorization before AI executes planning operations.","System Architecture & Design: Exclude AI from architectural and complex business-logic review decisions."],"table_practices":["Have AI surface assumptions and rejected alternatives for confirmation before proceeding","Keep final design and planning decisions behind human sign-off"],"practices":[{"name":"Have AI surface assumptions and rejected alternatives for confirmation before proceeding","definition":"Before implementation, the AI asks questions to confirm its assumptions, surfaces hidden load-bearing assumptions, and explains the alternatives it rejected, so unstated requirements reach a person rather than being inferred.","includes":"Custom design assistance, confirmation questions, hidden assumptions, and rejected alternatives.","excludes":"General comparisons.","quotes":[{"text":"I would use the concept but not the text. Though I would more language to disambiguate the meaning of change, maintian, etc. […] surfacing hiddent load bearing assumptions is a must but also asking it to surface considered alternatives and why it did not choose an alternative can help surface unspecified elements that you may not see otherwise.","who":"PF27"}]},{"name":"Keep final design and planning decisions behind human sign-off","definition":"AI may research, organize, propose, and draft, but a person owns consequential design and planning choices and approves the final result.","includes":"Explicit human ownership, approval, or final decision-making after AI input.","excludes":"Human approval before artifact generation.","quotes":[{"text":"AI can accelerate early planning, but its suggestions should be validated by engineers against business needs, technical constraints, and long-term maintainability.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"AI is most valuable when its work is transparent, testable, and easy to review. Human accountability should remain central, especially for consequential technical decisions","who":"MS117"},{"text":"Architecture wise, it often takes a lot of rounds discussion with copilot to collect info, value pros and cons and make trade off decision. Those decisions are still made by human as of today","who":"MS164"},{"text":"I would use AI as a support tool for exploring ideas and planning, while keeping important decisions human-led. […] AI can make design and planning more efficient, while human judgment remains important for key decisions.","who":"PF40"},{"text":"Across all topics, the one thing I always come back to is: never let AI have the final say; humans need to be the final sign-off on all work product, including meta-work. I scold AI all the time for acting without me instructing it to.","who":"MS30"},{"text":"for some of these things (ex. performance, sec decisions), it is good to brainstorm with AI and figure out the options with various assumptions and let the human make the final decisions abased on the criteria. AI can make suggestions.","who":"MS110"},{"text":"The items prohibiting and limiting the use of LLMs seem the most useful and impactful. Using LLMs to \"convert reviewed designs into downstream artifacts\" and \"to explore design and architecture alternatives\" is already heavily used and can be genuinely useful in some cases.","who":"MS211"},{"text":"Using multiple agents instead of a singular agent is key here. Use a anent for initial design, then use other agents to verify, ask questions, find flaws in the design which you as a SME can interact with. Repeat until new agents find no new flaws and you as a human agree with the overall design.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"These guidelines reflect how I want to use AI: strong support for research, synthesis, and routine execution, while humans retain accountability for strategy, stakeholder decisions, architecture, and work assignments. Reviewability, provenance, uncertainty, and easy rollback should be standard requirements rather than optional features.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"In design and planning I use AI to find and synthesize context from multiple sources — prior test plans, specs, and past regression results — to shape an approach and surface expected trade-offs before I commit to it. I demand it disclose its scope and assumptions and hold the consequential decisions for my sign-off, so the AI accelerates the exploration but I own the final plan.","who":"MS45"}]}]},{"id":"DP7","category":"DP","kind":"limit","text":"Limit AI's role in strategic vision setting, stakeholder alignment, and human-relational work.","scenarios":["Vision: AI can provide input, but setting product vision stays human-led.","Stakeholder alignment: AI can summarize each side's position, but a person navigates the actual alignment and negotiation.","Team dynamics: AI supports with insights; humans handle the interpersonal side."],"origins":["Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Preserve human accountability and interaction in requirements gathering.","System Architecture & Design: Preserve human ownership of long-term vision and stakeholder alignment.","Task & Project Planning / Management: Exclude AI from empathy-dependent coordination, including vision-setting, stakeholder alignment, and team-dynamic interventions."],"table_practices":["Bring AI in to manage the work only after people have set the strategic direction and decided what to build"],"practices":[{"name":"Bring AI in to manage the work only after people have set the strategic direction and decided what to build","definition":"People decide what the project should pursue before AI joins to manage or elaborate the chosen work.","includes":"Delaying AI involvement until strategic vision becomes a concrete implementation decision.","excludes":"Technical sign-off after AI exploration.","quotes":[{"text":"I would ask AI to help me directly manage a project once we've gone from strategic vision to an actual decision on what to implement.","who":"MS22"}]}]},{"id":"QR1","category":"QR","kind":"use","text":"Use AI for continuous, proactive QA that understands the project's context; demand AI to surface findings ranked by risk, linked to evidence, and paired with reviewable fix guidance.","scenarios":["Testing/QA: AI continuously flags the riskiest issues in the codebase, links each to the code that caused it, and proposes a fix. The developer uses the ranking and evidence to decide which to merge now and which to defer.","Security: A developer asks AI to scan a service for vulnerabilities. AI ranks findings by severity, shows the evidence for each, and drafts a reviewable PR. The developer rejects one fix as riskier than the bug and merges the rest.","Code review: At PR time, AI points out a likely edge-case bug, explains why it is risky with a link to the line, and proposes a change. The reviewer judges it against rollout impact before accepting."],"origins":["Security & Compliance: Support continuous, proactive security risk detection with prioritization and fix guidance to surface deviations from baselines.","Security & Compliance: Automate security test generation including edge case, adversarial, and chaos testing from code context.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Provide locally grounded, product-aware review feedback that draws on provenance and context to the right documentation.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Assess and triage changes (diff, PR) by risk level to prioritize human reviewer attention.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Generate tests and refactor in tandem with code review at PR submission.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Generate context-aware tests from code, specs, and natural language, paying attention to edge/risk cases.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Analyze coverage gaps and prioritize test creation by risk.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Integrate proactive risk or prioritized anomaly detection, root cause analysis, quality gates, and regression alerting detection.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Conduct security adversarial, chaos, edge case, and exploratory testing (with detection and fixes) with reproducible artifacts.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Enforce proactive quality assurance and testing alongside every performance optimization suggestion, enabling developers to validate and approve."],"table_practices":["Continuously scan code, telemetry, and releases to catch emerging risks","Demand that AI rank its findings by risk to focus limited review effort","Investigate logs and failures with AI to reproduce issues and propose fixes","Demand evidence and citations behind every AI quality finding","Assess testing gaps from code, change, and coverage data","Use AI during pull-request review to flag risks and gaps"],"practices":[{"name":"Continuously scan code, telemetry, and releases to catch emerging risks","definition":"AI watches the product throughout development and around releases, surfacing regressions, vulnerabilities, testing gaps, and quality problems for people to address.","includes":"Regular, continuous, or release-triggered scans of code, telemetry, dependencies, or product quality.","excludes":"Post-merge checks that route alerts or bugs to an owner","quotes":[{"text":"Ask the AI to monitor for code changes to be deployed into Test\\Env run pre-agreed verification steps and provide final report including evidence, citations.","who":"MS85"},{"text":"Continuous improvement and optimization of our product would benefit from a provable improvement in shipping quality driven by AI and run continuously against our codebase.","who":"MS82"},{"text":"I use AI for continuous, context-aware QA in my projects, ranking potential issues by risk and linking each finding to evidence and a proposed fix. A real person must review and approve findings rather than letting AI certify its own work.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"Having something constantly doing proactive QA seems great, and is aligned with what we've already been doing on my team to an extent (e.g. we constantly have AI e2e test new features or do a full round of testing before each release we ship).","who":"MS8"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"Having AI continually QA products before shipping could avoid bugs making their way to production.","who":"MS192"},{"text":"'- Automate quality/risk analysis checks in my project on a regular cadence and surface the results that humans can review,","who":"MS178"}]},{"name":"Demand that AI rank its findings by risk to focus limited review effort","definition":"AI turns broad inspection into a prioritized list of likely risks or actionable issues so people can spend scarce review or audit time where it matters most.","includes":"Risk-ranked findings, audit backlogs, and triage that directs manual review effort.","excludes":"Proof supporting a finding","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI for continuous, context-aware QA in my projects, ranking potential issues by risk and linking each finding to evidence and a proposed fix. A real person must review and approve findings rather than letting AI certify its own work.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"It would help me focus where I need to spend my effort of manual review.","who":"MS148"}]},{"name":"Investigate logs and failures with AI to reproduce issues and propose fixes","definition":"AI reads logs, crash dumps, test results, or build breaks, compares them with code, reproduces the problem, and recommends a repair.","includes":"Log search, crash analysis, local reproduction, test-result analysis, and build-break diagnosis that stops at a proposed fix.","excludes":"AI that proceeds to implement and test the repair","quotes":[{"text":"Perform log search, compare with code to ensure executions are happening as expected, pull error logs, investigate. Pull crash dump details, investigate and apply fixes","who":"MS49"},{"text":"I would like AI to assist me with analyzing test results, identifying solutions for build breaks, discovering variants of security issues, supporting debugging efforts, and reviewing code.","who":"MS96"}]},{"name":"Demand evidence and citations behind every AI quality finding","definition":"Before acting, the developer asks AI to connect each finding to concrete evidence, citations, a reviewable fix, or pre-agreed verification steps.","includes":"Explicit demands for evidence, citations, proof, or traceable support for AI quality claims.","excludes":"Independent tests used as the acceptance gate","quotes":[{"text":"ask AI for evidence. use other AI agents/models to review outputs + human review. AI needs context, just throwing AI at things will result in subpar result.","who":"MS110"},{"text":"I use AI for continuous, context-aware QA in my projects, ranking potential issues by risk and linking each finding to evidence and a proposed fix. A real person must review and approve findings rather than letting AI certify its own work.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"Ask the AI to monitor for code changes to be deployed into Test\\Env run pre-agreed verification steps and provide final report including evidence, citations.","who":"MS85"}]},{"name":"Assess testing gaps from code, change, and coverage data","definition":"AI reasons over code, change history, test results, and coverage data to identify thinly tested paths, regression risks, or related security gaps.","includes":"Testing-gap analysis and risk assessment grounded in code, changes, coverage, or known issue variants.","excludes":"Writing tests to close identified gaps","quotes":[{"text":"I've used it to assess testing gaps, either by straight reasoning over code, churn by PRs, or looking at our code coverage data.","who":"MS197"},{"text":"I would like AI to assist me with analyzing test results, identifying solutions for build breaks, discovering variants of security issues, supporting debugging efforts, and reviewing code.","who":"MS96"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"Risk assessment of security fixes. Writing test coverage. Regression Risk Assessment.","who":"MS55"}]},{"name":"Use AI during pull-request review to flag risks and gaps","definition":"A developer uses AI while reviewing a pull request to summarize scope, probe functionality, and identify quality or security issues before accepting the change.","includes":"AI pre-scans and review assistance focused on pull requests, especially other developers’ changes.","excludes":"Review of one's own uncommitted work","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to assist with code review of other engineering PRs. Using AI to summarize the scope of changes, and ask it questions so it can investigate functionality gaps, etc is very useful.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"I do use AI to 'pre-scan' code that I'm reviewing to identify security issues, usually in parallel with myself also doing it. Then I compare its results and reasoning with my own, effectively being a 'second pair of eyes'","who":"MS154"},{"text":"Doing code review for multiple GitHub pull requests and reviewing local code changes in depth. […] Additional context tweaks from human on non documented things on code or docs. And not assume things before confirmation which are not clear.","who":"PF18"},{"text":"Using the AI to validate PRs and determine if there are risks related to it","who":"MS15"}]},{"name":"Let AI investigate signals and autonomously implement and test fixes","definition":"AI starts from logs, telemetry, dashboards, or its own product understanding, then authors, builds, deploys, and tests a repair without a person initiating every step.","includes":"End-to-end autonomous investigation and execution of fixes from operational signals.","excludes":"Investigation that stops at a recommendation","quotes":[{"text":"Perform log search, compare with code to ensure executions are happening as expected, pull error logs, investigate. Pull crash dump details, investigate and apply fixes","who":"MS49"}]},{"name":"Feed AI project context and review history before trusting findings","definition":"Developers provide codebase knowledge, coverage information, instructions, or prior review choices so AI findings reflect the actual project rather than generic guesses.","includes":"Project context, coverage data, change history, review history, and instructions supplied to improve quality findings.","excludes":"Rules that deliberately limit sensitive-data access","quotes":[{"text":"I will also add like “self learn based on the history of the reviews made by me in past and follow the principles”","who":"MS175"},{"text":"ask AI for evidence. use other AI agents/models to review outputs + human review. AI needs context, just throwing AI at things will result in subpar result.","who":"MS110"}]},{"name":"Run post-merge agents that alert owners or file bugs","definition":"Agents watch landed or recently pushed changes and notify the responsible developer or file a bug when they detect a failure or regression.","includes":"Post-merge checks, monitoring of recent pushes, and alerts or bug filing tied to a change owner.","excludes":"Broader standing scans without owner-directed alerting","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI to monitor changes pushed by me recently and alert if I cause any failures or regressions so I can proactively work towards it.","who":"MS135"}]},{"name":"Ask AI to fix CI-only failures, harden brittle tests, and probe attack vectors","definition":"Use AI when tests pass locally but fail in automated checks, and to improve unreliable tests, uncover bugs and security weaknesses, and reorganize test support code. Ask for fresh ideas when unsure what to try next.","includes":"Troubleshooting, test improvements, security investigation, and seeking ideas.","excludes":"Broader troubleshooting.","quotes":[{"text":"Main use: fix CI failures that pass locally Secondary use: reduce test brittleness, find new attack vectors, surface bugs, refactor test helpers […] AI is most useful when I'm at a loss on what to do next and need someone to think outside the box I'm currently stuck in.","who":"PF20"}]}]},{"id":"QR2","category":"QR","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to automate routine QA and compliance tasks; demand AI to provide uncertainty indicators on every output.","scenarios":["Compliance: A developer asks AI to check code against accessibility standards. AI reports what passed and failed and marks how confident it is in each call.","Testing: A developer asks AI to generate unit tests for a module. AI writes them and flags the ones it's unsure actually capture the intended behavior.","Code review: A developer has AI do a first-pass review for common mistakes. AI comments on what it checked and notes its confidence, so humans know where to look harder."],"origins":["Security & Compliance: Automate routine compliance and standard-enforcement checks - with confidence indicators and human validation before submission.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Do compliance checking with confidence scores on every PR.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Enable team-configurable custom review agents for repetitive review tasks with guardrails in PR workflows.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Enforce fail-loud behavior, explicit confidence indicators, and signaling when there is insufficient context or AI is not confident."],"table_practices":["Automate recurring compliance checks, alerts, and result routing","Assign neglected QA backlogs to AI when teams lack capacity","Run end-to-end tests through browser automation and test infrastructure","Have AI write tests while developers retain testing ownership"],"practices":[{"name":"Automate recurring compliance checks, alerts, and result routing","definition":"AI or ordinary automation handles mechanical compliance work, recurring alerts, and the movement of results into governance or tracking systems.","includes":"SOX work, accessibility checks, compliance-alert handling, result plumbing, and recurring compliance monitoring.","excludes":"An explicit boundary that reserves compliance judgment for people","quotes":[{"text":"I would use AI to support routine quality checks and highlight potential risks while making sure the results are reviewed by a human. […] AI can be very useful for identifying issues early but human review is still important, especially when there is uncertainty.","who":"PF40"},{"text":"We are already doing this to some extent. From making it add tests that have full coverage of any new changes, to automating UI so we can run e2e integration tests. Some accessibility checks can be automated (like color contrast ratios) but we normally go through human review for compliance and such.","who":"MS19"},{"text":"Run quality control validations and code quality reports to improve overall application output.","who":"PF25"}]},{"name":"Assign neglected QA backlogs to AI when teams lack capacity","definition":"Teams give AI testing, audit, analysis, or implementation work that otherwise would not be done because people lack time or capacity.","includes":"Untested-code backlogs, audit queues, recurring checks, and other quality work explicitly framed as a capacity gap.","excludes":"Routine test creation without a capacity shortfall","quotes":[{"text":"Using a generative system to produce reports of likely risks is a defensible way to produce an audit backlog …","who":"MS65"}]},{"name":"Run end-to-end tests through browser automation and test infrastructure","definition":"AI drives browsers, integration environments, test plans, stubs, or simulators to exercise features and run full test passes with less manual effort.","includes":"AI-run UI, browser, integration, or end-to-end testing tied to operational test infrastructure.","excludes":"Building missing simulator infrastructure as a prerequisite","quotes":[{"text":"Having something constantly doing proactive QA seems great, and is aligned with what we've already been doing on my team to an extent (e.g. we constantly have AI e2e test new features or do a full round of testing before each release we ship).","who":"MS8"},{"text":"We are already doing this to some extent. From making it add tests that have full coverage of any new changes, to automating UI so we can run e2e integration tests. Some accessibility checks can be automated (like color contrast ratios) but we normally go through human review for compliance and such.","who":"MS19"}]},{"name":"Have AI write tests while developers retain testing ownership","definition":"AI drafts unit, integration, functional, UI, or coverage tests at varying scale, while developers remain responsible for deciding what testing is adequate.","includes":"AI-authored tests for current work, missing coverage, or untested areas, whether as a starting point or in bulk.","excludes":"Checking whether those tests genuinely exercise the intended behavior","quotes":[{"text":"I largely use the tool to do a deep quality analysis of the work being done. Producing enough tests and also finding gotchas.","who":"MS113"},{"text":"Directing AI to generate tests around the features it implements and checking that the tests behave as expected through AI generated tests.","who":"MS70"},{"text":"I use Copilot to write specific tests. I don't want to relegate all testing to AI. At the end of the day, I am responsible for the code and quality. I don't even let it commit code for me on my local repo. I do that myself.","who":"MS10"},{"text":"I currently ask copilot to help with unit tests. I do not let it push any changes even to my branch unless I have reviewed it, but it does help me with a starting place, and sometimes it comes up with something I have not thought of.","who":"MS90"},{"text":"We are already doing this to some extent. From making it add tests that have full coverage of any new changes, to automating UI so we can run e2e integration tests. Some accessibility checks can be automated (like color contrast ratios) but we normally go through human review for compliance and such.","who":"MS19"},{"text":"I do have AI generate tests. It is difficult to get it to both generate tests and also make sure that it is producing correct code. Often it's not a problem but sometimes there is a sneaky or dumb flaw with the test that means it is not testing what I thought it was testing. So you have to be careful.","who":"MS66"},{"text":"Risk assessment of security fixes. Writing test coverage. Regression Risk Assessment.","who":"MS55"}]}]},{"id":"QR3","category":"QR","kind":"limit","text":"Limit AI's handling of personally identifiable and sensitive compliance data to least-privilege access.","scenarios":["Compliance: AI is given only the minimum data it needs for a check, not the full customer database.","Security: A developer keeps real customer data and secrets out of AI's reach, using redacted or synthetic data instead.","Sensitive configs: When a task touches sensitive settings, AI is scoped to read only what's strictly necessary."],"origins":["Security & Compliance: Prohibit AI from directly handling personally identifiable or sensitive data - ensuring privacy safeguards and explicit authorization govern any data exposure (least privilege access)."],"table_practices":["Make clear which data and tools AI may use, and scope its access to sensitive data"],"practices":[]},{"id":"QR4","category":"QR","kind":"limit","text":"Prohibit AI from self-certifying the correctness or quality of its own output.","scenarios":["Testing: AI can generate code and tests, but it doesn't get to declare its own output correct; a human or an independent check validates it.","Quality: When AI writes a feature, a different (human) reviewer judges whether the result is good, not the same AI.","Standards: A developer has AI check work against standards but verifies the criteria themselves rather than letting AI grade itself."],"origins":["Testing & Quality Assurance: Prohibit AI from self-evaluating its own test artifacts or quality (e.g., correctness, coverage) without human review.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Require human sign-off on test intent/strategy, acceptance criteria, quality verification, and all AI-generated tests.","Code Review / Pull Requests: Surface risk signals and synthesize but reserve release-readiness and risk-acceptance judgments for human reviewers.","Testing & Quality Assurance: Prohibit AI from final release-readiness, triage decisions, and risk-acceptance judgments without human sign-off.","Security & Compliance: Prevent AI from issuing opaque security assurances without human review."],"table_practices":["Gate AI output with deterministic tests","Use automated builds to catch false claims in AI's reports of success","Check that AI-written tests exercise the intended behavior","Use a separate agent as a reviewer to check AI's contributions","Keep approval of AI findings, fixes, and commits with a human"],"practices":[{"name":"Gate AI output with deterministic tests","definition":"Concrete validation tests, rules, or pre-agreed checks determine whether AI-generated work is correct before check-in, independent of the AI's own assessment.","includes":"Deterministic tests, rule-based checks, evals, and fixed verification plans used as acceptance gates.","excludes":"Requests for citations supporting findings","quotes":[{"text":"AI can improve speed and early issue detection, but strong approval gates, audit trails, and rollback plans are essential. Humans should retain control of production changes, incident decisions, and stakeholder communication.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I want to have dev validation tests that ensure that scenarios don't break and that AI assumptions and updates are validated before we check in. Also, I want to have code review suggestions of what should be fixed or updated, but not requirements. A human should decide if code is good enough to check in, not an AI.","who":"MS39"},{"text":"Intentional human gates, enforced by actual governance tools (hooks, vs prompts) are critical","who":"MS179"}]},{"name":"Use automated builds to catch false claims in AI's reports of success","definition":"Let AI write and run tests, and trust its reports that tests ran successfully while relying on automated builds to catch false claims. This rejects a blanket prohibition on AI certifying its own results.","includes":"Test writing, execution, and qualified acceptance of AI's own success reports.","excludes":"The opposing position; sensitive data preferences.","quotes":[{"text":"AIs can and should write and run tests, but we trust them when they self-critify that they ran it and they succeeded. … build pipelines need to catch any liars.","who":"PF3"}]},{"name":"Check that AI-written tests exercise the intended behavior","definition":"Developers inspect and run AI-generated tests to ensure they test the intended behavior rather than merely pass or hide a defect.","includes":"Explicit checks of AI-written test correctness, expected behavior, and whether test changes mask the real problem.","excludes":"Creating tests in the first place","quotes":[{"text":"Directing AI to generate tests around the features it implements and checking that the tests behave as expected through AI generated tests.","who":"MS70"},{"text":"I do have AI generate tests. It is difficult to get it to both generate tests and also make sure that it is producing correct code. Often it's not a problem but sometimes there is a sneaky or dumb flaw with the test that means it is not testing what I thought it was testing. So you have to be careful.","who":"MS66"}]},{"name":"Use a separate agent as a reviewer to check AI's contributions","definition":"Work produced by one AI is evaluated by a distinct agent, model, skill, harness, or human review process rather than accepted on the producer's judgment.","includes":"Separate checking agents, models, skills, harnesses, or parallel reviewers.","excludes":"Using fixed tests as the decisive gate","quotes":[{"text":"Human needs to understand what AI actually is testing against. Sometimes, AI change the test to make the test pass rather than fix the real code bug","who":"MS164"},{"text":"ask AI for evidence. use other AI agents/models to review outputs + human review. AI needs context, just throwing AI at things will result in subpar result.","who":"MS110"},{"text":"If the same context model that does development does the validation and writes unit test cases - most times all test cases are always passing so not sure if the testing covers all cases.","who":"MS206"},{"text":"I find it difficult to predict what tasks AI will be good at and what tasks it will try to cheat at. Because of this it requires lots of supervision-- I find it often needs MORE supervision for things like rebuilding than it does for writing complex algorithms.","who":"MS66"},{"text":"Using multiple agents instead of a singular agent is key here. Use a anent for initial design, then use other agents to verify, ask questions, find flaws in the design which you as a SME can interact with. Repeat until new agents find no new flaws and you as a human agree with the overall design.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"AI can improve the breadth and speed of quality checks, but it should never be treated as proof that its own work is correct. Access to sensitive data should be narrowly scoped, and confidence levels must be supported by evidence rather than presented as unsupported scores. Final validations can be automated but needs to be deterministic.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"While we wouldn't let the AI correct and approve its own PRs, we sometimes use one agent to create the code and another person(s) run(s) their own agents to review the PR. As long as different people's criteria are taken into account this is good. You can also ask the agent to do a 'code quality improvement' pass or similar before releasing the change.","who":"MS19"},{"text":"I even use different harnesses for code reviews that helps catch things I missed.","who":"MS171"},{"text":"Use AI to actively build and test. Test is mostly done by other skills rather than the same agent certifying itself","who":"MS162"},{"text":"subagents with clean context and from different family of models to review the ouptut of the other to be more unbiased","who":"PF15"}]},{"name":"Keep approval of AI findings, fixes, and commits with a human","definition":"A person reviews AI findings, suggested fixes, or quality claims and decides whether they count and whether code is staged, committed, or checked in; AI never certifies its own output.","includes":"Human approval of findings, fixes, quality claims, commits, pushes, and check-in readiness.","excludes":"A distinct checking mechanism (another agent, model, harness, or deterministic tests).","quotes":[{"text":"I currently ask copilot to help with unit tests. I do not let it push any changes even to my branch unless I have reviewed it, but it does help me with a starting place, and sometimes it comes up with something I have not thought of.","who":"MS90"},{"text":"When I make unstaged changes, I am making it a habit to ask AI to review it for style and correctness before staging and committing my changes. I will always review the generated feedback myself to make or approve any suggested changes.","who":"MS200"},{"text":"I use AI for continuous, context-aware QA in my projects, ranking potential issues by risk and linking each finding to evidence and a proposed fix. A real person must review and approve findings rather than letting AI certify its own work.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"I would use AI to support routine quality checks and highlight potential risks while making sure the results are reviewed by a human. […] AI can be very useful for identifying issues early but human review is still important, especially when there is uncertainty.","who":"PF40"},{"text":"I would use AI to continuously review code and changes for testing gaps, regressions, security risks, and compliance issues. It should rank findings by impact, link each finding to concrete evidence, suggest a reviewable fix, and leave final validation to independent tests and human reviewers.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I want to have dev validation tests that ensure that scenarios don't break and that AI assumptions and updates are validated before we check in. Also, I want to have code review suggestions of what should be fixed or updated, but not requirements. A human should decide if code is good enough to check in, not an AI.","who":"MS39"},{"text":"'- Automate quality/risk analysis checks in my project on a regular cadence and surface the results that humans can review,","who":"MS178"},{"text":"ask AI for evidence. use other AI agents/models to review outputs + human review. AI needs context, just throwing AI at things will result in subpar result.","who":"MS110"},{"text":"I use Copilot to write specific tests. I don't want to relegate all testing to AI. At the end of the day, I am responsible for the code and quality. I don't even let it commit code for me on my local repo. I do that myself.","who":"MS10"}]}]},{"id":"IO1","category":"IO","kind":"use","text":"Use AI for continuous, telemetry-driven DevOps monitoring, analysis, and release-readiness assessment; demand AI to provide review-ready summaries and pre-incident warnings.","scenarios":["Monitoring: A developer has AI watch metrics and logs. AI surfaces anomalies before they become incidents and explains what it's seeing so the on-call engineer can act.","Customer support: A developer has AI triage incoming support tickets. AI buckets them by likely cause using the ticket text and logs, for a human to confirm.","Release readiness: Before a release, a developer asks AI to assess readiness. AI summarizes what changed, which tests and checks passed or failed, and the open risks (each linked to its evidence), and leaves the go/no-go decision to the release owner."],"origins":["DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Automate telemetry-driven triage, anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and deployment-risk assessment with human review.","Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Telemetry-driven anomaly detection, root-cause analysis, and failure prediction.","Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Telemetry-driven synthesis of incident context, observability, and incident response for on-call engineers.","Customer Support: Correlate support ticket spikes with telemetry to detect systemic issues."],"table_practices":["Continuously monitor telemetry for anomalies and emerging failures","Correlate logs and telemetry to diagnose incidents and surface likely causes","Triage every incoming incident before human handling","Watch deployments and releases through completion","Triage failed tests and assess release quality","Report patterns and trends across support incidents"],"practices":[{"name":"Continuously monitor telemetry for anomalies and emerging failures","definition":"Agents watch environments, tenants, alerts, heartbeats, and operational signals continuously to detect trouble early and reduce manual watching.","includes":"Always-on monitoring, anomaly detection, alert intake, environment health checks, and early warning.","excludes":"Watching a named release; diagnosing a known incident.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze telemetry and draft Infrastructure-as-Code changes, requiring clear explanations and a human-readable diff. A human would review and approve every production change before execution","who":"MS117"},{"text":"I'd love to implement the anomaly monitoring. AI should not modify production (or non-production!) environments, critical (or non-critical!) infrastructure, security configurations, or access controls on its own.","who":"MS127"},{"text":"Currently there AI is not that much good in continuous monitoring of incident and warning of system failure, it would be good to have AI continuous telemetry/other sources driven monitoring which can help in early detection/ mitigation of issue.","who":"MS149"},{"text":"The main win in ops is speed of diagnosis — AI can pull together logs, stats, and asset state and explain what likely went wrong with source-linked evidence I can verify. But in this domain the blast radius is high, so I want it to be explicit about assumptions and uncertainty and to keep any change that touches shared or production systems behind an explicit human approval gate.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Environment Telemetry Monitoring and Risk/Pattern Classification","who":"MS61"},{"text":"should facilitate autonomous monitoring and quicker root cause determination","who":"MS64"}]},{"name":"Correlate logs and telemetry to diagnose incidents and surface likely causes","definition":"AI gathers operational evidence from several sources into one incident picture, identifies patterns, and points engineers toward likely root causes.","includes":"Diagnosing a known incident or [incident system] by combining logs, monitors, statistics, support history, or service telemetry.","excludes":"Standing first-pass intake; early warning.","quotes":[{"text":"I use AI tooling to gather data from many sources of telemetry for our service, combining into a single picture to derive RCA for [incident tickets] and issues.","who":"MS126"},{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze monitoring data, summarize incidents, identify likely root causes, and suggest remediation steps so engineers can respond faster.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"It has been useful in debugging and triaging incidents since it can synthesize large amounts of data from logs, monitors, etc and quickly identify patterns and key issues.","who":"MS18"},{"text":"Read lot's of logs, setup thorough logging, co-design the architecture and and plan improvements or maintainance or audit for recent vulnerabilities […] ai is a tool and we have similar concepts to implement like firewalls and sandbox boundaries and role/identity management. these are to be applied to ai to manage them securely","who":"PF15"},{"text":"Use AI for continuous, telemetry-driven DevOps monitoring, analysis, and release-readiness assessment; demand AI to provide review-ready summaries and pre-incident warnings. -- This is very useful as input. AI can correlate data from multiple sources effectively. What it misses often is the interpretation, so review-ready summaries with confidence level clearly described is very useful.","who":"MS125"},{"text":"In infra and ops I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like triaging regression/CI failures, correlating logs and stats, and drafting runbook steps — under human oversight, with the AI producing a reviewable plan of action before anything runs. For consequential or destructive operations (config changes, deployments, resets) I demand it hold for my sign-off rather than applying them itself.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"RCA of [incident tickets]. Update ARM template.","who":"MS153"},{"text":"use AI to help diagnosis k8s deployment issue etc, [monitoring system] logging issue at infra layer","who":"MS92"}]},{"name":"Triage every incoming incident before human handling","definition":"AI takes the first pass on each incoming alert or [incident system], producing an initial diagnosis, next steps, or a transfer target before an engineer engages.","includes":"Standing procedures that put AI first in the incoming incident or alert queue.","excludes":"Deeper evidence correlation after pickup.","quotes":[{"text":"We use AI to triage incoming alerts","who":"MS109"}]},{"name":"Watch deployments and releases through completion","definition":"AI follows pipeline runs, deployments, releases, or test-environment status so engineers do not have to refresh dashboards or babysit progress.","includes":"Monitoring a specific deploy, release, pipeline run, or environment heartbeat through completion.","excludes":"General early-warning monitoring; authoring pipeline definitions.","quotes":[{"text":"streamlining the creation, monitoring, and maintenance of CI/CD pipelines","who":"MS48"}]},{"name":"Triage failed tests and assess release quality","definition":"AI reviews failed tests, test-pass results, and release-quality reports to give engineers an initial assessment before manual investigation.","includes":"Initial triage of regression or CI failures and assessment of release test results.","excludes":"Carrying failures through remediation; aggregate trend reporting.","quotes":[{"text":"Analyze testpass results and release quality reports.","who":"MS159"},{"text":"In infra and ops I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like triaging regression/CI failures, correlating logs and stats, and drafting runbook steps — under human oversight, with the AI producing a reviewable plan of action before anything runs. For consequential or destructive operations (config changes, deployments, resets) I demand it hold for my sign-off rather than applying them itself.","who":"MS45"}]},{"name":"Report patterns and trends across support incidents","definition":"AI examines incident histories, test-pass results, and release-quality reports as a body to surface recurring patterns, trends, and risks.","includes":"Periodic or aggregate analysis across multiple support incidents or batches of quality data.","excludes":"Single-incident diagnosis; individual failed-test triage.","quotes":[{"text":"We use AI to … provide reports to see patterns and trends in support.","who":"MS5"}]}]},{"id":"IO2","category":"IO","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to generate Infrastructure-as-Code and configuration workflows from intent; demand AI to provide a human-readable diff before executing changes.","scenarios":["DevOps: A developer asks AI to add a caching step to their CI/CD pipeline. AI writes the config change and shows a natural language diff of what it will do, holding until the developer approves.","Infrastructure-as-code: A developer describes a new service they need stood up. AI generates the Terraform and presents the exact resources it will create and modify as a readable diff before applying.","Environment setup: A developer asks AI to bump a memory limit in their staging config. AI produces the edit as a diff showing the old and new values, and waits for sign-off before it takes effect."],"origins":["DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Create, repair, improve, CI/CD pipeline configurations from high-level intent.","Environment Setup & Maintenance: Automate environment provisioning, configurations upgrade management and infrastructure migration into a cohesive workflow.","Environment Setup & Maintenance: Automate cross-platform compatible environment script generation.","DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Automate repetitive operational toil with reversible previews for human review."],"table_practices":["Generate infrastructure-as-code from high-level intent","Demand a reviewable diff or plan before infrastructure changes run","Build and maintain CI/CD pipelines from stated requirements"],"practices":[{"name":"Generate infrastructure-as-code from high-level intent","definition":"AI translates a higher-level requirement into Terraform, ARM, [deployment system], or related infrastructure artifacts and can revise existing templates.","includes":"Creating or updating infrastructure-as-code and deployment artifacts from requirements or declared intent.","excludes":"Pipeline definitions; standards checks.","quotes":[{"text":"AI for IaC work, building pipelines and ensuring it's compliant with respective standards and policies following the best practices.","who":"MS162"},{"text":"Using AI in conjunction with infrastructure as code like Terraform is helpful, basically acting as one level higher of the declarative intent for infra as code.","who":"MS47"},{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze telemetry and draft Infrastructure-as-Code changes, requiring clear explanations and a human-readable diff. A human would review and approve every production change before execution","who":"MS117"},{"text":"RCA of [incident tickets]. Update ARM template.","who":"MS153"}]},{"name":"Demand a reviewable diff or plan before infrastructure changes run","definition":"AI presents a proposed infrastructure change as a pull request, readable diff, or clear plan, and a person reviews it before execution.","includes":"Review artifacts and explicit pre-execution approval for AI-authored infrastructure or configuration changes.","excludes":"General production execution controls; confidence on recommendations.","quotes":[{"text":"AI is most valuable as a copilot—not a replacement for human judgment. Recommendations should be explainable, auditable, and require approval before making production changes.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze telemetry and draft Infrastructure-as-Code changes, requiring clear explanations and a human-readable diff. A human would review and approve every production change before execution","who":"MS117"},{"text":"The main win in ops is speed of diagnosis — AI can pull together logs, stats, and asset state and explain what likely went wrong with source-linked evidence I can verify. But in this domain the blast radius is high, so I want it to be explicit about assumptions and uncertainty and to keep any change that touches shared or production systems behind an explicit human approval gate.","who":"MS45"}]},{"name":"Build and maintain CI/CD pipelines from stated requirements","definition":"Engineers give AI a pipeline definition or repository plus a requirement, and AI builds, changes, fixes, or maintains the pipeline.","includes":"Authoring, revising, or repairing CI/CD pipeline definitions, including iterative pull requests.","excludes":"Infrastructure templates; watching pipeline runs.","quotes":[{"text":"streamlining the creation, monitoring, and maintenance of CI/CD pipelines","who":"MS48"},{"text":"daily CI/CD development and scheduling work items which would reqguire multiple PR iterations","who":"MS9"},{"text":"AI for IaC work, building pipelines and ensuring it's compliant with respective standards and policies following the best practices.","who":"MS162"},{"text":"I usually create a PR to update the pipeline so we can deploy our product in the test harness. I’ll share the link to the pipeline definition and ask the AI to make the changes based on my requirement.","who":"MS23"},{"text":"I use AI to build the pipelines and fix the issues in pipelines","who":"MS33"}]}]},{"id":"IO3","category":"IO","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to automate routine operations tasks; demand AI to provide uncertainty indicators and clear, traceable explanations on every recommendation.","scenarios":["Environment setup: A developer asks AI to script provisioning for a new staging environment. AI writes it, and its trace shows one step assumes a fixed region, so the developer parameterizes it before rolling out.","DevOps: A developer asks AI to recommend a fix for a failing pipeline. AI gives the fix, shows the reasoning and the data behind it, and flags how confident it is.","Dependency maintenance: A developer asks AI to bump outdated packages. AI proposes the upgrades and marks low confidence on one major-version bump whose changelog hints at a breaking API, so the developer pins it for manual review."],"origins":["DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Automate repetitive operational toil with reversible previews for human review.","DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Expose step-by-step rationale for every AI-generated infrastructure recommendation to support engineer skill development.","DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Preserve operational intuition and understanding by exposing AI reasoning for pipelines.","Customer Support: Automate support ticket screening, triage, and known-issue deflection.","Customer Support: Equip support representatives with knowledge retrieval.","Environment Setup & Maintenance: Require traceability: execution logs, provenance metadata, and test artifacts, for all AI-applied changes."],"table_practices":["Draft remediation steps, fixes, and runbooks for engineers","Convert remaining manual operations into supervised scripts and workflows","Wire SRE agents to incident triggers, telemetry, and scoped action tools","Require reviewable summaries, confidence, and explanations before letting AI make production changes"],"practices":[{"name":"Draft remediation steps, fixes, and runbooks for engineers","definition":"After diagnosis, AI proposes concrete mitigations, transfer targets, runbook steps, or a draft fix while engineers choose and apply the response.","includes":"Recommendations or authored fixes that remain proposals for an operator to assess.","excludes":"Automatic repair; diagnosis alone.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze monitoring data, summarize incidents, identify likely root causes, and suggest remediation steps so engineers can respond faster.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"In infra and ops I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like triaging regression/CI failures, correlating logs and stats, and drafting runbook steps — under human oversight, with the AI producing a reviewable plan of action before anything runs. For consequential or destructive operations (config changes, deployments, resets) I demand it hold for my sign-off rather than applying them itself.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Automatically diagnose and triage incoming incidents - suggest next steps, potential mitigations, transfer targets, etc.","who":"MS172"}]},{"name":"Convert remaining manual operations into supervised scripts and workflows","definition":"AI analyzes repetitive or legacy operational work and drafts scripts, runbook steps, or supervised workflows that people can run at scale.","includes":"Turning manual release or operations steps into scripts or supervised automation.","excludes":"AI executing consequential changes; failure remediation.","quotes":[{"text":"There are a few remaining manual operations related to releases. … Automation is a goal and AI use in analyzing and adapting manual routine tasks is a strong use case.","who":"MS82"}]},{"name":"Wire SRE agents to incident triggers, telemetry, and scoped action tools","definition":"Teams stand up an SRE agent or Copilot skill with incident triggers, Kusto or [monitoring system] access, runbooks, and team knowledge so routine investigations can run as a workflow.","includes":"Concrete agent-host setups naming triggers, operational data tools, action tools, or a team knowledge base.","excludes":"The intake practice; widening authority.","quotes":[{"text":"SREAgent or alike host, with several input/triggers - [incident system], [incident system] workflow; kusto MCP and probably scoped/filtered [monitoring system] Actions as tools for starters.","who":"MS165"}]},{"name":"Require reviewable summaries, confidence, and explanations before letting AI make production changes","definition":"AI turns operational evidence into a reviewable account, states confidence, and explains the basis of recommendations so engineers can judge them.","includes":"Review-ready summaries, explicit confidence levels, and understandable explanations attached to analysis or recommendations.","excludes":"Readable infrastructure diffs; root-cause verification.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze monitoring data, summarize incidents, identify likely root causes, and suggest remediation steps so engineers can respond faster.","who":"MS88"},{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze telemetry and draft Infrastructure-as-Code changes, requiring clear explanations and a human-readable diff. A human would review and approve every production change before execution","who":"MS117"},{"text":"Use AI for continuous, telemetry-driven DevOps monitoring, analysis, and release-readiness assessment; demand AI to provide review-ready summaries and pre-incident warnings. -- This is very useful as input. AI can correlate data from multiple sources effectively. What it misses often is the interpretation, so review-ready summaries with confidence level clearly described is very useful.","who":"MS125"},{"text":"In infra and ops I use AI to automate routine, verifiable tasks — like triaging regression/CI failures, correlating logs and stats, and drafting runbook steps — under human oversight, with the AI producing a reviewable plan of action before anything runs. For consequential or destructive operations (config changes, deployments, resets) I demand it hold for my sign-off rather than applying them itself.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"Mostly used for explanations about the logs and summaries","who":"MS215"}]},{"name":"Have AI assess alerts, attempt fixes, and escalate what it cannot resolve","definition":"Give AI the first attempt at assessing and resolving monitoring alerts, escalating alerts it cannot resolve.","includes":"The alert assessment, attempted resolution, and escalation arrangement.","excludes":"Rejection of prohibitions. Log explanation alone.","quotes":[{"text":"AI performing first-pass triage and attempted resolution for alerts from monitoring tools, wth proper escalation if it cannot resolve it.","who":"PF24"}]}]},{"id":"IO4","category":"IO","kind":"limit","text":"Prohibit AI from modifying production environments, critical infrastructure, security configurations, or access controls on its own.","scenarios":["Production: AI can prepare a change, but it never pushes to production by itself; a human executes.","Access control: AI doesn't touch permissions or secrets; it can read and alert only.","Critical infrastructure: For infra changes, AI proposes and a person applies."],"origins":["DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Gate all production deployments, live configuration changes, releases, and security-decisions on human approval.","Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Prohibit autonomous AI actions on production infrastructure and critical actions.","Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Exclude AI from access/security/secrets permission management.","Environment Setup & Maintenance: Exclude AI from modifying security configuration and access-control changes.","Quality & Risk, Security & Compliance: Gate all security & compliance changes on human approval."],"table_practices":["Fence AI with firewalls, sandboxes, and identity controls while it helps run infrastructure","Hold consequential environment changes for human approval or execution"],"practices":[{"name":"Fence AI with firewalls, sandboxes, and identity controls while it helps run infrastructure","definition":"Apply the same security boundaries used for any tool (firewalls, sandbox boundaries, role and identity management) to the AI, and within them use it to set up logging, co-design architecture, plan maintenance and audit for recent vulnerabilities.","includes":"The logging setup, architecture design, maintenance planning, vulnerability checks, and security controls.","excludes":"Log interpretation. Opposition to prohibitions.","quotes":[{"text":"Read lot's of logs, setup thorough logging, co-design the architecture and and plan improvements or maintainance or audit for recent vulnerabilities […] ai is a tool and we have similar concepts to implement like firewalls and sandbox boundaries and role/identity management. these are to be applied to ai to manage them securely","who":"PF15"}]},{"name":"Hold consequential environment changes for human approval or execution","definition":"AI may prepare scripts, configuration changes, deployments, or remediation steps, but a person decides whether consequential or production actions run.","includes":"Human sign-off, human execution, or a standing human gate for production and other consequential changes.","excludes":"Diff-specific infrastructure review; autonomous carve-outs.","quotes":[{"text":"I’d use AI to analyze telemetry and draft Infrastructure-as-Code changes, requiring clear explanations and a human-readable diff. A human would review and approve every production change before execution","who":"MS117"},{"text":"The main win in ops is speed of diagnosis — AI can pull together logs, stats, and asset state and explain what likely went wrong with source-linked evidence I can verify. But in this domain the blast radius is high, so I want it to be explicit about assumptions and uncertainty and to keep any change that touches shared or production systems behind an explicit human approval gate.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"AI is most useful as an integrated copilot with access to relevant context, while engineers remain accountable for validating decisions and production changes.","who":"MS88"}]},{"name":"Auto-remediate bounded routine failures without case-by-case approval","definition":"Agents autonomously repair already-broken assets, retry transient errors, or resolve known-safe issues within a preapproved scope.","includes":"Unsupervised remediation justified by low risk, a bounded scope, or the inability to make an already-broken item worse.","excludes":"Mandatory human approval; gradual authority expansion.","quotes":[{"text":"I want to use these to detect and correct transient infrastructure errors including retries without necessarily hiding the underlying issue but unblocking workflows while notifying stakeholders about the infrastructure issues.","who":"MS202"},{"text":"AI is most valuable when it can work autonomously within clear boundaries while keeping its reasoning, assumptions, sources, and changes visible. Human review should focus on risk and intent rather than requiring approval for every routine step.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"These practices would make AI output easier to trust, review, and audit. The requirements should be applied proportionally, with stricter evidence and approval standards for higher-risk work.","who":"MS117"}]},{"name":"Expand agent production rights gradually through scoped tools","definition":"Teams start agents with filtered actions or limited authority and widen production rights step by step as workflows earn trust.","includes":"Incremental grants of authority, scoped action tools, and planned expansion toward fuller autonomy.","excludes":"Fixed low-risk carve-outs; independent watchdog designs.","quotes":[{"text":"SREAgent or alike host, with several input/triggers - [incident system], [incident system] workflow; kusto MCP and probably scoped/filtered [monitoring system] Actions as tools for starters.","who":"MS165"}]}]},{"id":"IO5","category":"IO","kind":"limit","text":"Prohibit AI from autonomously communicating with stakeholders or making emergency-response decisions.","scenarios":["Customer comms: AI doesn't reach out to customers on its own; a human approves and sends.","Emergency response: During an outage, AI can inform and suggest, but a person makes the response decisions.","High-impact actions: For anything that could cause serious downtime, AI never acts autonomously; the human pulls the trigger."],"origins":["Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Retain human judgement and autonomy for all emergency and business-impact decisions.","Customer Support: Prohibit AI from sending customer-facing communications autonomously, particularly those requiring empathy or contextual judgment."],"table_practices":["Assign a person accountable for emergency decisions and messages sent under their name: route emergency decisions to a human, check messages before they are sent, and let agents report only routine release status"],"practices":[]},{"id":"MW1","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to draft stakeholder communications adapted to each audience's needs and intended call to action; demand AI to provide drafts traceable to their source materials.","scenarios":["Stakeholder comms: A developer turns their progress notes into an update for a non-technical sponsor. AI adapts the detail to what the sponsor needs and states the decision it's asking them for, with each point linked to the tickets behind it.","Reporting: A developer asks for a release announcement for two audiences. AI writes a detailed version for the engineering channel and a higher-level one for leadership, each with its own call to action, and cites the PRs it drew from.","Onboarding: A developer asks for a welcome doc for a new hire. AI adapts it to what the new hire needs on day one, points them to their first task, and links back to the team docs it used."],"origins":["Client/Stakeholder Communication: Automate rote communication tasks with editable draft outputs for human review.","Client/Stakeholder Communication: Provide communication clarity and accessibility support for different stakeholder audiences.","Client/Stakeholder Communication: Synthesize project contexts to surface meeting summaries, status reports, decisions, and coordination gaps."],"table_practices":["Draft routine communication from material developers supply, and keep interpersonal messages their own: AI drafts newsletters from recent updates and summarizes them, while developers write to colleagues themselves"],"practices":[{"name":"Draft routine communication from material developers supply, and keep interpersonal messages their own: AI drafts newsletters from recent updates and summarizes them, while developers write to colleagues themselves","definition":"Use AI to help draft a newsletter from recent updates.","includes":"Providing recent updates for AI to turn into a newsletter draft.","excludes":"Rejecting AI-written text.","quotes":[{"text":"help draft a newsletter given recent updates","who":"PF26"}]},{"name":"Write interpersonal messages yourself, limiting AI to summaries and proofreading","definition":"Keep people as the recognizable authors of messages to colleagues and stakeholders, using AI only for summary, grammar, spelling, or other mechanical help.","includes":"Refusing AI-written interpersonal messages, preserving human voice, or limiting AI to proofreading and summarization.","excludes":"AI drafts that people substantially review and rewrite; logistical automation.","quotes":[{"text":"These questions are worded clearly, but improperly. For example: \"Use AI to adapt learning to knowledge level and project needs;\" -- Yes, go ahead, take the knowledge that we have crafted for you and tell me which part of that knowledge is relative to what I am asking for. \"demand AI to scaffold active, hands-on practice and deliberate thinking.\" -- Absolutely NOT, I do not want AI interfering with the brainstorming or design processes, or acting as a guide. Design and discussion should be human-led. Another one: \"Use AI to automate routine communication and collaboration logistics;\" -- Using AI to establish Rhythm of Business meetings and planning sessions (collaboration logistics) is fine. Communication? (that leads me to...) \"demand AI to never dispatch consequential stakeholder-facing messages without approval.\" -- AI should not be involved in communications in any way. Human beings are, as a species, social. We are communal. Involving AI in communications remove one of the last few things that make us human.","who":"MS115"}]}]},{"id":"MW2","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to automate routine communication and collaboration logistics; demand AI to never dispatch consequential stakeholder-facing messages without approval.","scenarios":["Stakeholder email: A developer has AI handle routine inbox replies. AI answers a teammate's scheduling question on its own, and holds a reply to a client about a slipped deadline for approval.","Scheduling meetings: A developer has AI manage their calendar. AI books a recurring internal sync from their standing preferences and sends the invite itself, but when moving a delivery review that external partners attend, it holds the reschedule for approval before sending.","Onboarding: A developer has AI set up a new hire. AI sends the standard first-week logistics itself, and holds the announcement introducing the hire to the wider org until the developer approves it."],"origins":["Client/Stakeholder Communication: Automate rote communication tasks with editable draft outputs for human review.","Client/Stakeholder Communication: Synthesize project contexts to surface meeting summaries, status reports, decisions, and coordination gaps.","Client/Stakeholder Communication: Retain human authenticity and oversight for stakeholder communication."],"table_practices":["Track work and compile stakeholder updates from ADO and Teams","Draft release notes and routine announcements from commits, then review before sending"],"practices":[{"name":"Track work and compile stakeholder updates from ADO and Teams","definition":"Connect AI to work-tracking and discussion systems so it can follow open work, assemble status, and help keep stakeholders informed.","includes":"Tracking ADO tickets, using AI skills with ADO, or drawing status context from Teams and work records.","excludes":"General meeting and thread summaries; reminders without message generation.","quotes":[{"text":"I already use AI to keep track of my ADO tickets to keep stakeholders informed.","who":"MS20"},{"text":"AI is a constant thought partner as I am working out complex concepts in building applications. It has been great at providing comprehensive documentation on my development project and also helping me to document my work each sprint. I use it extensively to recap meetings and research topics in Teams where are multiple threads as well. I use AI skills on my projects to interact with ADO.","who":"MS5"}]},{"name":"Draft release notes and routine announcements from commits, then review before sending","definition":"Use commits, code changes, or release material to draft release notes, partner announcements, or routine newsletters that a person reviews and rewrites before they go out.","includes":"Release notes, GitHub release communications, and other routine release announcements generated from code history.","excludes":"READMEs and PR descriptions; reviewed leadership drafts.","quotes":[{"text":"Creating release notes from commits Creating PR descriptions from code changes","who":"MS107"},{"text":"Using AI to automate routine communication could be helpful for communicating releases.","who":"MS192"},{"text":"we use this to generate GitHub release communication","who":"MS23"}]}]},{"id":"MW3","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to adapt learning to knowledge level and project needs; demand AI to scaffold active, hands-on practice and deliberate thinking.","scenarios":["Learning: A developer new to Kubernetes asks how to get a service running. AI works from what they already know about Docker, explains the new concepts on top of that, and gives them a manifest to apply themselves.","Learning in project context: A developer learning a framework their team uses gets examples written against their own project's code, and works through building one real feature with it rather than a generic sample app.","Research & brainstorming: A developer facing a new design problem uses AI to brainstorm approaches. AI offers options and tradeoffs; the developer thinks through which fits and prototypes it."],"origins":["Learning New Technologies: Provide adaptive learning scaffolding for each developer's knowledge level and learning style.","Learning New Technologies: Facilitate practice-based learning exercises that require active learner engagement.","Learning New Technologies: Prohibit AI from short-circuiting hands-on learning experience.","Research & Brainstorming: Adapt research support to the developer's knowledge and project context."],"table_practices":["Summarize and filter trusted learning resources around the learner's question","Have AI explain each code line and write your own learning notes"],"practices":[{"name":"Summarize and filter trusted learning resources around the learner's question","definition":"Use AI to collate existing resources, summarize technical documents, and select the parts of established knowledge relevant to the learner's question.","includes":"Document summaries, resource collation, and relevance filtering used as inputs to learning.","excludes":"AI acting as the teacher; meeting summaries.","quotes":[{"text":"I would use AI to help me understand some technology by summarizing the corresponding documents.","who":"MS84"},{"text":"I am unsure that the accuracy and reliability of AI as a teacher is there yet especially if you are learning something that you can not verify because you are a novice in the area. AI can collate resources, but relying on AI that halucinates to teach is a dangerous step.","who":"MS90"}]},{"name":"Have AI explain each code line and write your own learning notes","definition":"Ask AI to explain its outputs for learning as well as checking correctness. Understand each line of code and write personal notes separately from project goals.","includes":"AI explanations paired with understanding each code line and writing notes for personal learning.","excludes":"Finding and summarizing resources. Reviewing shared documentation.","quotes":[{"text":"Always have AI explain outputs not only for validation but for learning purposes, making sure I understand each line of code and writing notes for my own understanding, separate to project goals","who":"PF23"}]}]},{"id":"MW4","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI as a thought partner to challenge and refine ideas; demand AI to defer final decisions to human judgment.","scenarios":["Brainstorming: A developer proposes handling retries with a fixed delay. AI flags that it can overload the server during an outage and offers backoff with jitter as an option, leaving the choice to the developer.","Research: A developer wants GraphQL for a new service. AI lays out where REST would be simpler for their read-heavy case, and defers the decision to them","Learning: A developer learning React assumes a global store is the right home for all their state. AI questions that default and shows where local component state would be simpler."],"origins":["Learning New Technologies: Prohibit AI from short-circuiting hands-on learning experience.","Research & Brainstorming: Provide brainstorming and ideation support for rapid exploration and prototyping.","Research & Brainstorming: Preserve developer intellectual engagement with research and ideation tasks.","Research & Brainstorming: Prohibit AI from making or endorsing final research or design decisions."],"table_practices":["Bring ideas to AI for questions, challenge, and refinement","Set up dedicated AI projects and personalize settings for thinking through ideas","Use AI to help find/summarize resources"],"practices":[{"name":"Bring ideas to AI for questions, challenge, and refinement","definition":"Start with a human-originated idea, then use AI as an ongoing thought partner to question assumptions, surface missed points, and sharpen the idea.","includes":"Conversational brainstorming, pushback, iterative questions, and refinement of an idea supplied by the person.","excludes":"Formal robustness testing tied to sources; refusing AI participation.","quotes":[{"text":"\"Use AI as a thought partner to challenge and refine ideas\". I do that all the time. I start with an idea, and AI refines it.","who":"MS7"},{"text":"I've used it to push back on my ideas in general and iterate with questions. Sometimes it brings up good points I didn't think of before","who":"MS197"},{"text":"AI is a constant thought partner as I am working out complex concepts in building applications. It has been great at providing comprehensive documentation on my development project and also helping me to document my work each sprint. I use it extensively to recap meetings and research topics in Teams where are multiple threads as well. I use AI skills on my projects to interact with ADO.","who":"MS5"}]},{"name":"Set up dedicated AI projects and personalize settings for thinking through ideas","definition":"Use dedicated AI projects for regularly thinking through ideas with AI. Pay attention to personalization and project settings.","includes":"Dedicated projects and attention to their settings for ongoing discussions with AI.","excludes":"Finding and summarizing resources.","quotes":[{"text":"I use thought partnership all the time. […] Pay attention to personalization and project settings, use dedicated projects for through partnership.","who":"PF27"}]},{"name":"Use AI to help find/summarize resources","definition":"Work with AI to improve how you find resources and summarize them.","includes":"Using AI as a thinking partner for resource discovery and summaries.","excludes":"Configuring projects for regular discussions.","quotes":[{"text":"Thought partner for improve resource discovery and summarizing.","who":"PF25"}]}]},{"id":"MW5","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to curate, validate, and maintain documentation and shared project context; demand AI to preserve fidelity to its sources and flag stale or conflicting context for human review.","scenarios":["Docs drafting: A developer asks AI to document a module from the code, PRs, and tests. AI drafts it and links each section to the source it came from.","Docs maintenance: A developer asks AI to reconcile a service's README, ADRs, and inline comments after a refactor. AI updates each, links every change to the code it reflects, and flags two that now contradict each other for a human to resolve.","Docs validation: A developer asks AI to check whether the onboarding guide still matches the codebase. AI lists the steps that no longer match, cites the code that diverged, and proposes edits without publishing them."],"origins":["Documentation: From project artifacts, auto-generate context-aware synthesis, maintenance, and updates of documentation with human approval.","Documentation: Provide project knowledge search, indexing, and discovery with provenance.","Documentation: Preserve human oversight, authenticity, and vetting before any documentation is published or distributed."],"table_practices":["Update and validate shared documentation as work evolves","Scan documentation against code and changes to flag drift"],"practices":[{"name":"Update and validate shared documentation as work evolves","definition":"Use AI to keep project documentation and shared knowledge current, including incorporating updates and documenting completed sprint work.","includes":"Validating, updating, maintaining, or adding ongoing work to existing project documentation and shared knowledge.","excludes":"Finding discrepancies without rewriting; creating new code-derived artifacts.","quotes":[{"text":"It could be use to create and maintain a common/shared knowledge base.","who":"MS21"},{"text":"AI is a constant thought partner as I am working out complex concepts in building applications. It has been great at providing comprehensive documentation on my development project and also helping me to document my work each sprint. I use it extensively to recap meetings and research topics in Teams where are multiple threads as well. I use AI skills on my projects to interact with ADO.","who":"MS5"}]},{"name":"Scan documentation against code and changes to flag drift","definition":"Compare current documentation and troubleshooting guides with code or ongoing changes to find stale, inconsistent, or diverged material.","includes":"Staleness scans, consistency checks, and detection of drift between documented behavior and current code.","excludes":"Rewriting and routine upkeep.","quotes":[{"text":"Having AI analyze documents and TSGs to verify its consistency with ongoing changes","who":"MS131"},{"text":"It would be very nice to occasionally scan project documentation for stale/out of date items.","who":"MS2"}]}]},{"id":"MW6","category":"MW","kind":"use","text":"Use AI to create personalized training plans and materials for onboarding and learning new technologies; demand AI to provide editable, source-linked drafts.","scenarios":["Onboarding: A developer asks AI to build a training plan for a new hire. AI generates a sequenced plan tailored to the hire's role and current level that the developer can edit, with links to the source docs.","Learning path: A developer asks AI for a plan to learn a new technology. AI produces steps and materials matched to what the developer already knows, each pointing to where it came from.","New-hire resources: A developer asks AI to gather setup resources for onboarding. AI assembles editable, source-linked materials, leaving the human connection to the mentor."],"origins":["Mentoring & Onboarding: Support knowledge transfer through onboarding, walkthroughs, and searchable resource retrieval.","Learning New Technologies: Provide adaptive learning scaffolding for each developer's knowledge level and learning style."],"table_practices":["Generate up-to-date training and onboarding materials: wiki pages for knowledge transfer, guides to new AI tools adapted to the learner's skill, and overviews of features owned by other teams"],"practices":[{"name":"Generate up-to-date training and onboarding materials: wiki pages for knowledge transfer, guides to new AI tools adapted to the learner's skill, and overviews of features owned by other teams","definition":"Use AI to prepare wiki pages, feature overviews, training content, scenarios, and onboarding paths suited to the learner's starting point and skill level.","includes":"Reusable training or onboarding artifacts, knowledge-transfer wikis, scenario questions and answers, and learner-tailored materials or paths.","excludes":"Interactive codebase exploration; general project documentation.","quotes":[{"text":"Using AI to create training / onboarding materials about new AI tools would be beneficial, and adapting those materials to skill level will prevent wasted time","who":"MS160"},{"text":"Using AI to build learning and tools for learning/onboarding sounds like a great idea. I would use to this generate up-to-date wiki material for knowledge transfer.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"Training materials and Overview on some features that are not ours. Scenarios Q&A","who":"MS177"}]}]},{"id":"MW7","category":"MW","kind":"limit","text":"Limit AI's role in mentoring to preserve mentor and mentee development and tacit knowledge transfer.","scenarios":["Code review: AI catches mechanical issues (style, obvious bugs); the senior reviewer gives the judgment-level feedback on a junior's PR to transfer tacit knowledge (\"why we do it this way here\") that never gets taught explicitly.","Onboarding: AI runs the checklist (entitlements, cloning a repo, first build); the mentor tells the new hire which service is a minefield, which doc is stale, and who to actually ask.","Mentee growth: for a stuck mentee, AI hands over resources; the mentor asks what they've tried, so both the mentee and mentor develop."],"origins":["Mentoring & Onboarding: Prohibit AI from serving as the mentor for new hires; require mentor oversight for all AI-generated guidance.","Mentoring & Onboarding: Preserve mentoring opportunities for mentor development."],"table_practices":["Keep mentoring and people decisions human: face-to-face time and whiteboarding with new hires, messages to colleagues written by people, and AI brought in afterward for implementation and checking"],"practices":[]},{"id":"CC1","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to ask for clarification instead of acting on missing or inferred context.","scenarios":["Coding: If a request is ambiguous, AI asks a clarifying question instead of guessing and building the wrong thing.","Planning: When key context is missing, AI pauses and asks rather than filling the gap with a random assumption.","Design: AI says the request isn't clear enough and asks, rather than handing back an incomplete or broken solution."],"origins":["Design & Planning, Task & Project Planning / Management: Block plan generation when required grounding data is unavailable, identify missing inputs.","Design & Planning, Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Restrict AI from originating or inferring unstated requirements.","Development, Coding/Programming: Facilitate multi- and cross-context coding support, flag when full scope is unavailable."],"table_practices":["Make AI ask before ambiguous work begins: instruct it to ask about intent or open choices rather than guess, surface unknowns and assumptions for confirmation, and clarify feedback before acting on it"],"practices":[{"name":"Make AI ask before ambiguous work begins: instruct it to ask about intent or open choices rather than guess, surface unknowns and assumptions for confirmation, and clarify feedback before acting on it","definition":"Surface consequential unknowns and consult the user or a subject-matter expert before acting on missing context.","includes":"Standing ask-first instructions, clarification of received feedback, and naming missing scenarios requiring expert input.","excludes":"Risk-gated assumptions; evidence-first investigation.","quotes":[{"text":"AI should never allowed to guess, but since we are using probabilistic models to give us the best guess of what the next token will be will make it hard to decide what \"correct\" means.","who":"MS90"},{"text":"The AI tool must try to ask as much as questions needed in the beginning that I myself tell it ask me if any questions. I often have to add ask me anything you need before jumping to solutions.","who":"MS113"},{"text":"I support all of these principles, but clarification should be risk-based rather than mandatory whenever any context is missing. Excessive questions can interrupt work, while silent assumptions on consequential decisions create unacceptable risk.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I think demanding AI ask for clarification often stops autonomous work and costs more in productivity than simply fixing incorrect assumptions afterwards. I do want AI not to make wrong assumptions, but I think the bar should be high to avoid unnecessary clarifications.","who":"MS80"},{"text":"AI often \"loses it's mind\" and goes for overly complex solutions, or doesn't ask questions when appropriate to find the best solution. We need to ensure AI is remember that there are human subject matter experts it can rely on for additional context not initially given to it.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"In the early times this year AI used to gave half baked answer which is now really improved and would love to have some backing reasoning when AI make an analysis or judgement or clearly state that 'I might be missing xyx scenario which is not mentioned in this repo and need to che ck with someone'","who":"MS9"},{"text":"I would use AI throughout my work to explore solutions and understand new problems, while reviewing its suggestions and making sure it does not make assumptions when context is missing. […] AI can be really useful across software engineering, but it is important to understand and review what it produces rather than relying on it blindly.","who":"PF40"},{"text":"Having LLMs clearly stated as co-authors of generated code which developers accept would go far. A sort of water-marking might be needed in the future to determine clearly what is LLM generated code or not. I have a few sub agents / skills where I clearly state for the agent to surface unknowns and assumptions. Then to get input from the user on those before performing any next steps.","who":"MS14"},{"text":"Use AI to ask for clarification on feedback received, to avoid misunderstanding before working on changes.","who":"MS11"},{"text":"Sometimes there is ambiguity in my request and instead of clarifying, the AI implements something completely incorrect.","who":"MS2"}]},{"name":"Proceed on low-risk assumptions and report them","definition":"Act on reasonable assumptions for reversible work, disclose them with the result, and stop when ambiguity could materially affect outcomes.","includes":"Proceed-and-report behavior and explicit security, architecture, cost, or user-impact thresholds.","excludes":"Unconditional clarification.","quotes":[{"text":"AI is most valuable when it can work autonomously within clear boundaries while keeping its reasoning, assumptions, sources, and changes visible. Human review should focus on risk and intent rather than requiring approval for every routine step.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I think it's helpful for AI to make assumptions and act on inferred context as long as those assumptions are pointed out along with the result. That gives us the efficiency of the human not having to answer a ton of questions while allowing them to correct the output when necessary, when a bad assumption affects the result.","who":"MS52"},{"text":"These practices would make AI output easier to trust, review, and audit. The requirements should be applied proportionally, with stricter evidence and approval standards for higher-risk work.","who":"MS117"}]}]},{"id":"CC2","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to prefer simple, deliberate solutions over superficial fixes or unnecessarily complex ones.","scenarios":["Coding: Before writing new code, AI checks whether a standard library or an existing method already does the job.","Design: AI proposes the simplest design that meets the need instead of an over-engineered one.","Bug fixing: AI traces a bug to its root cause and fixes that, instead of adding a superficial workaround that masks the symptom."],"origins":["Design & Planning, Requirements Gathering & Analysis: Explore design, technology, and architecture options with structured trade-off analysis.","Development, Coding/Programming: Preserve learning, skill-building, and craftsmanship."],"table_practices":["Repeatedly trim unnecessarily complex code and verbose documentation","Choose the leanest complete root-cause fix","Pull AI back from over-broad changes","Expect AI to avoid unnecessary complexity without being told"],"practices":[{"name":"Repeatedly trim unnecessarily complex code and verbose documentation","definition":"Enforce simple code and crisp documentation throughout the work, correcting complexity or verbosity as it appears.","includes":"Repeated reminders, continuous simplicity enforcement, and warnings against unreadable explanatory text.","excludes":"An end-of-task cleanup; root-cause selection.","quotes":[{"text":"I've found that AI sometimes introduces unnecessary complexity as well as overly verbose comments. My team and I prioritize simplicity. This is something that I consistent have to remind AI.","who":"MS94"},{"text":"AI often \"loses it's mind\" and goes for overly complex solutions, or doesn't ask questions when appropriate to find the best solution. We need to ensure AI is remember that there are human subject matter experts it can rely on for additional context not initially given to it.","who":"MS83"},{"text":"Most of my interaction with LLMs to day comes down to deciding what needs to be done, and catching the LLM when it goes down the wrong path (adding too much complexity or making shallow fixes instead of solving the bigger issue), so making the LLM more able to pick the right path to solve a task is very important.","who":"MS169"},{"text":"Demand AI to prefer simple, deliberate solutions over superficial fixes or unnecessarily complex ones. -- this is very important; the more AI code is produced, the more the standards become over complex and over documented code. Enforcing simple solutions and crisp documentation at every step can prevent this to become a problem at scale.","who":"MS125"}]},{"name":"Choose the leanest complete root-cause fix","definition":"Solve the underlying problem with the least complicated complete change instead of layering superficial patches.","includes":"Root-cause analysis, smallest-complete-solution instructions, and rejection of shallow fixes.","excludes":"Constraining an over-broad edit.","quotes":[{"text":"I require AI to prefer the smallest complete solution that addresses the root cause, rather than layering on quick patches or unnecessary complexity. This keeps changes easier for engineers to review, test, and maintain.","who":"MS12"},{"text":"Most of my interaction with LLMs to day comes down to deciding what needs to be done, and catching the LLM when it goes down the wrong path (adding too much complexity or making shallow fixes instead of solving the bigger issue), so making the LLM more able to pick the right path to solve a task is very important.","who":"MS169"},{"text":"Being able to review smaller chunks of AI work seems obviously good (as compared to large swaths of code), as is the \"prefer simple, deliberate solutions\" one. But as with some of the earlier stuff in this survey, the latter is a hard problem to solve-- e.g. what if the simple solution is a bandaid? what if doing 20% more work leads to a more elegant, lasting solution? does it matter what layer of the project the change is in?","who":"MS8"},{"text":"Demand AI to think and analyze the problem and find the solutions instead of a superficial fix. It has the ability to identify stale code/references and suggest better hardening solutions.","who":"MS162"}]},{"name":"Pull AI back from over-broad changes","definition":"Keep implementation within stated intent by stopping wide refactors, duplicated work, or invented features.","includes":"Mid-task redirection toward the minimal intended scope.","excludes":"General simplicity reminders; skeptic-agent checks.","quotes":[{"text":"Already trying to drive this today through skills … sometimes it attempts to strongly refactor large scopes of work rather than creating minimal changes.","who":"MS70"}]},{"name":"Expect AI to avoid unnecessary complexity without being told","definition":"Reject a separate instruction to avoid unnecessary complexity because this behavior should already be expected.","includes":"Objection that stating the simplicity demand should be unnecessary.","excludes":"Objecting because a problem requires complexity.","quotes":[{"text":"Just like \"Do not make mistakes\", you shouldn't need to specify avoiding unnecessarily complex solutions.","who":"PF20"}]},{"name":"Allow complex solutions when complex problems require them","definition":"Reject demanding simple solutions for complex problems because that demand does not work.","includes":"Objection to requiring simplicity despite problem complexity.","excludes":"Treating the instruction as unnecessary.","quotes":[{"text":"… Demanding simple solutions for complex problems does not work.","who":"PF3"}]}]},{"id":"CC3","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to safeguard the privacy and security of the data it handles, exposing or retaining it only as the task requires.","scenarios":["Security/Compliance: Fixing a config that holds credentials, AI reads only the values the task needs and keeps it out of logs, and commit messages.","Quality Assurance: Testing against internal team data, AI keeps that data inside the task: not passing it into an external service or holding onto it after the check finishes.","Stakeholder communication: Drafting an outward update, AI includes only what the audience is cleared to see, leaving confidential figures and internal identifiers out."],"origins":["Quality & Risk, Security & Compliance: Prohibit AI from directly handling personally identifiable or sensitive data - ensuring privacy safeguards and explicit authorization govern any data exposure (least privilege access).","Meta-work, Client/Stakeholder Communication: Enforce data privacy controls on client and stakeholder communication threads.","Infrastructure & Ops, Infrastructure Monitoring & Alerts: Exclude AI from access/security/secrets permission management."],"table_practices":["Make AI apply security and privacy rules","Keep confidential information outside the repository so AI cannot access it"],"practices":[{"name":"Make AI apply security and privacy rules","definition":"Direct or expect AI to honor tenanting, security, and privacy practices while producing its work.","includes":"Per-task security instructions and expectations that AI apply established rules unprompted.","excludes":"Checking human assumptions; human-only decisions.","quotes":[{"text":"I already try to decompose my tasks into digestible atomic chunks to maximize the context for the AI agent and for me personally to review every line of code. AI should also be at a point that it should know the best practices in security and privacy so that we as engineers can focus more on the algorithm development and research.","who":"MS155"}]},{"name":"Keep confidential information outside the repository so AI cannot access it","definition":"Make confidential information inaccessible to AI by keeping it outside the repository, rather than relying on an instruction to protect privacy.","includes":"Proposal to prevent access to confidential information.","excludes":"General quality precautions.","quotes":[{"text":"You shouldn't have to ask AI to safeguard your privacy, you should make it impossible for AI to access any confidential information (keep it out of the repository).","who":"PF20"}]}]},{"id":"CC4","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to back its claims with cited, verifiable evidence, and to clearly state its assumptions, reasoning, scope, and uncertainty.","scenarios":["Research: When AI answers a technical question, it cites its sources so the developer can verify instead of trusting a confident-sounding guess.","Planning: AI explains the reasoning and assumptions behind a recommendation, so the human can push back on gaps.","Learning: AI links to authoritative docs and flags where it's uncertain or possibly out of date."],"origins":["Meta-work, Learning New Technologies: Require verifiable provenance and confidence/recency indicators on all learning content.","Meta-work, Research & Brainstorming: Synthesize and collate information from sources with citations and recency indicators.","Quality & Risk, Security & Compliance: Prevent AI from issuing opaque security assurances without human review.","Quality & Risk, Testing & Quality Assurance: Produce verifiable artifacts — logs, traces, screenshots — for every AI-executed test action, with human sign-off.","Quality & Risk, Testing & Quality Assurance: Enforce fail-loud behavior, explicit confidence indicators, and signaling when there is insufficient context or AI is not confident."],"table_practices":["Cite specific code locations and source documents","State reasoning, assumptions, uncertainty, and missing scenarios","Codify evidence checks as standing agent rules"],"practices":[{"name":"Cite specific code locations and source documents","definition":"Attach function names, line numbers, links, or bibliographies so claims and proposed fixes can be traced.","includes":"Code pointers, source-document references, and explicit per-prompt requests for citations and reasons.","excludes":"Persistent evidence requirements; following citations.","quotes":[{"text":"I extensively use AI for code analysis and this works best when it references specific function names or line numbers. I use those to a) validate what the AI said is accurate and b) because the code is my job, not the AI.","who":"MS24"},{"text":"AI is most valuable when it can work autonomously within clear boundaries while keeping its reasoning, assumptions, sources, and changes visible. Human review should focus on risk and intent rather than requiring approval for every routine step.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"I want the work it does to be traceable and easy to reproduce/walk through. One thing that is hard today is remembering the why behind a choice (small/big) since being less hands-on while considering options/root-causing/etc can result in the process being less sticky in my mind.","who":"MS18"},{"text":"The biggest value is having AI synthesize context from scattered sources (logs, configs, docs) and explain it with traceable, source-linked reasoning so I can verify it. I want it to disclose its assumptions and flag uncertainty instead of sounding confident when it's guessing — that trust boundary is what makes me comfortable adopting it for day-to-day dev work.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"As I mentioned earlier, I often use AI to analyze multiple input documents (e.g. wiki pages) to provide a consolidated view of a topic. At the same time, I request that the AI also provide a 'bibliography' so that I can trace facts/statements back to the original document, in case I want to review it for more depth or to check its veracity or understanding of the source material. This helps greatly to cut back on hallucinated facts.","who":"MS154"},{"text":"Intent drift and auditability are critical","who":"MS179"},{"text":"I have to ask explicitly to cite its findings and also cite the reason for the proposed solution.","who":"MS113"},{"text":"I'd love to have some confirmations of the claims and solutions. Also, I'd love the idea of avoiding over-engineering or building something I didn't ask it to build.","who":"MS142"},{"text":"AI is most valuable when its work is transparent, testable, and easy to review. Human accountability should remain central, especially for consequential technical decisions","who":"MS117"},{"text":"AI is most valuable as a copilot—not a replacement for human judgment. Recommendations should be explainable, auditable, and require approval before making production changes.","who":"MS88"}]},{"name":"State reasoning, assumptions, uncertainty, and missing scenarios","definition":"Explain why conclusions follow, disclose what is assumed, distinguish confidence from confirmation, and identify possible gaps.","includes":"Backing reasoning, assumption lists, confidence notation, uncertainty flags, and evidence-grounded plans.","excludes":"Stopping to ask; source pointers alone.","quotes":[{"text":"Cited verifiable evidence, stated assumptions, etc. is always useful when planning how to implement a feature. This helps to ensure that the resulting plan is grounded in reality.","who":"MS22"},{"text":"Most of these cover a need for improved human-in-the-loop collaboration with AI and agentic tools. Understanding of the confidence, evidence-chains, and human-scoped work units are all important parts of effective use of AI tooling with confidence.","who":"MS82"},{"text":"overconfidence of AI tools nowadays is concerning - so many occasions when AI claims were false ones. Like 'this issue is resolve. Not assumed but confirmed' - you test yourself and it is not resolved. So, having some embedded rules or notations to reduce that noise would be beneficial.","who":"MS142"},{"text":"In the early times this year AI used to gave half baked answer which is now really improved and would love to have some backing reasoning when AI make an analysis or judgement or clearly state that 'I might be missing xyx scenario which is not mentioned in this repo and need to che ck with someone'","who":"MS9"},{"text":"I would require AI to make small, reviewable changes supported by source links, tests, and clearly stated assumptions. For low-risk and reversible work, it should proceed using reasonable assumptions and report them; it should ask for clarification when ambiguity could materially affect security, architecture, cost, or user outcomes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"When ideating with AI in a possible use case I want it to back it's design decisions and explain me the approach it is taking","who":"PF13"},{"text":"I’d require AI to produce small, reviewable changes with clear assumptions and evidence. If important context is missing, it should ask before acting rather than guessing.","who":"MS117"}]},{"name":"Codify evidence checks as standing agent rules","definition":"Install citation and verification requirements in agent instructions or workflows so claims require evidence by default.","includes":"Hard evidence rules, reference methods, and reusable verification workflows.","excludes":"One-off citation requests.","quotes":[{"text":"I use almost all of these already. I have rigorous \"reference\" methodologies baked into my agentic workflows, so all content is backed by reproducible citable data. I have AI also mark all its content as AI-generated and human reviewed.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"overconfidence of AI tools nowadays is concerning - so many occasions when AI claims were false ones. Like 'this issue is resolve. Not assumed but confirmed' - you test yourself and it is not resolved. So, having some embedded rules or notations to reduce that noise would be beneficial.","who":"MS142"}]}]},{"id":"CC5","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to deliver its output in atomic, self-contained pieces a human can review.","scenarios":["Coding: AI submits a change as a series of small, self-contained PRs, each doing one thing, rather than one large refactor a reviewer can't take apart.","Operations: AI proposes a multi-step operational change as separate, individually reviewable actions instead of one bundled script.","Any artifact: AI breaks a long output into pieces that each stand on their own, so a human can review and approve them one at a time."],"origins":["Development, Refactoring & Maintenance: Propagate multi-file refactoring changes with an explicit impact map, with human review.","Development, Refactoring & Maintenance: Require explicit human approval for structural or bulk refactoring changes.","Quality & Risk, Testing & Quality Assurance: Enforce proactive quality assurance and testing alongside every performance optimization suggestion, enabling developers to validate and approve.","Quality & Risk, Code Review / Pull Requests: Prohibit autonomous code commits, test check-ins, and PR merges; require human sign off before any PR is marked review-complete.","Infrastructure & Ops, DevOps / CI-CD / Deployment: Automate repetitive operational toil with reversible previews for human review.","Design & Planning, Task & Project Planning / Management: Require human authorization before AI executes planning operations.","Meta-work, Client/Stakeholder Communication: Automate rote communication tasks with editable draft outputs for human review."],"table_practices":["Require AI to deliver atomic, reviewable changes","Decompose work yourself and verify each step","Give AI a guide with examples people have checked for easy review","Review project plans against requirements and add notes about the whole plan","Split AI output into pieces so team members can share review"],"practices":[{"name":"Require AI to deliver atomic, reviewable changes","definition":"Prompt or instruct AI to return digestible increments that humans can understand and inspect for design problems.","includes":"Small human-scoped outputs, incremental delivery rules, and reviewability-based change splitting.","excludes":"Human pre-decomposition; larger contextual reviews.","quotes":[{"text":"This is very similar to the previous response where I mentioned breaking down work into human-digestible changes so we can spot architectural issues instead of trawling through a maze of enormous changesets.","who":"MS47"},{"text":"Most of these cover a need for improved human-in-the-loop collaboration with AI and agentic tools. Understanding of the confidence, evidence-chains, and human-scoped work units are all important parts of effective use of AI tooling with confidence.","who":"MS82"},{"text":"I would require AI to make small, reviewable changes supported by source links, tests, and clearly stated assumptions. For low-risk and reversible work, it should proceed using reasonable assumptions and report them; it should ask for clarification when ambiguity could materially affect security, architecture, cost, or user outcomes.","who":"MS76"},{"text":"Being able to review smaller chunks of AI work seems obviously good (as compared to large swaths of code), as is the \"prefer simple, deliberate solutions\" one. But as with some of the earlier stuff in this survey, the latter is a hard problem to solve-- e.g. what if the simple solution is a bandaid? what if doing 20% more work leads to a more elegant, lasting solution? does it matter what layer of the project the change is in?","who":"MS8"},{"text":"Across engineering work I lean on AI to make sense of unfamiliar code and diagnose problems, and to automate the repetitive-but-verifiable parts — refactors, test scaffolding, log analysis — always in human-reviewable increments with traceable, source-linked explanations. It speeds me up on the routine and the context-gathering, while I stay the reviewer and keep the consequential and security- or performance-critical decisions for myself.","who":"MS45"},{"text":"I’d require AI to produce small, reviewable changes with clear assumptions and evidence. If important context is missing, it should ask before acting rather than guessing.","who":"MS117"},{"text":"Some of these statements (or well the spirit of it) I inject into my prompt to ensure that I get the outputs in increments I desire, and more thorough/cleaner output from AI.","who":"MS197"}]},{"name":"Decompose work yourself and verify each step","definition":"Have the developer divide work into bounded parts, hand them to AI, and check each part before continuing.","includes":"Human-led task decomposition, line-by-line review, and stepwise verification.","excludes":"AI-delivered increments.","quotes":[{"text":"During the building of the prototype we deliberately started with a very simple feature and asked the AI iteratively to develop new features on the previously working code. The AI was instructed to build a test harness to ensure that there was no regression when a new feature was added.","who":"MS103"},{"text":"I already try to decompose my tasks into digestible atomic chunks to maximize the context for the AI agent and for me personally to review every line of code. AI should also be at a point that it should know the best practices in security and privacy so that we as engineers can focus more on the algorithm development and research.","who":"MS155"},{"text":"I usually work with AI in small increments. It keeps things on track.","who":"MS127"}]},{"name":"Give AI a guide with examples people have checked for easy review","definition":"Provide a coding guide alongside every task, using examples written and checked by people. The examples show code organized into parts that are simple to review.","includes":"Task-accompanying guide showing coding style, structure, and reviewable parts.","excludes":"Repeated simplicity reminders.","quotes":[{"text":"Create a stylistic guide of good coding practices that I pass to AI alongside any task to provide examples of coding style and structure to follow. These guidelines involve human-written and human-validated code that is modular and simple to review","who":"PF23"}]},{"name":"Review project plans against requirements and add notes about the whole plan","definition":"Generate and review complex project plans against detailed requirements. Add notes about the whole plan alongside reviews of individual pieces to catch issues those reviews miss.","includes":"Plan generation and review, including notes that connect the individual pieces.","excludes":"Rejecting small outputs for large problems.","quotes":[{"text":"Generate and review complex project plans as per detailed requirements. […] Along with atomic self contained ones, sometimes full picture notes also needed, during which some issues not caught in atomic rounds show up.","who":"PF18"}]},{"name":"Split AI output into pieces so team members can share review","definition":"Divide AI output into individually reviewable pieces so human review work can be shared among team members.","includes":"Explicit connection between separate output pieces and distributing review.","excludes":"Adding notes about the whole plan.","quotes":[{"text":"atomic pieces help split human review work across team members","who":"PF26"}]},{"name":"Reject small output requirements for problems too large for that approach","definition":"Reject demanding small outputs for big problems because the approach does not work at that size.","includes":"Rejection of small output demands for large problems.","excludes":"Supplementing individual reviews while retaining them.","quotes":[{"text":"… Demanding small outputs for big problems does not scale.","who":"PF3"}]}]},{"id":"CC6","category":"CC","kind":"demand","text":"Demand AI to clearly annotate its generated contributions and communications to distinguish them from human-authored content.","scenarios":["Coding: When AI writes code, it labels it (e.g., an 'AI-used' tag on the PR) so reviewers know to look more carefully.","Documentation: AI marks the sections it generated so humans can tell them apart from human-written text.","Architecture flow: AI flags its own contributions (e.g., an architecture flowchart) rather than blending them invisibly into human work."],"origins":["Development, Coding/Programming: Make all AI-generated code artifacts identifiable and undergo mandatory human review.","Meta-work, Client/Stakeholder Communication: Retain human authenticity and oversight for stakeholder communication."],"table_practices":["Mark AI's contributions where reviewers see them: label AI-written review comments, mark generated content as AI-generated and human-reviewed, and credit AI as co-author on accepted code"],"practices":[{"name":"Mark AI's contributions where reviewers see them: label AI-written review comments, mark generated content as AI-generated and human-reviewed, and credit AI as co-author on accepted code","definition":"Attach visible AI attribution to generated code, content, or review comments, and record that a person reviewed, accepted, or finally approved the artifact.","includes":"AI bylines, watermarks, automated-reviewer notes, co-author credit, and human-reviewed stamps.","excludes":"Using provenance to calibrate reading; marking human work.","quotes":[{"text":"I use almost all of these already. I have rigorous \"reference\" methodologies baked into my agentic workflows, so all content is backed by reproducible citable data. I have AI also mark all its content as AI-generated and human reviewed.","who":"MS120"},{"text":"Having LLMs clearly stated as co-authors of generated code which developers accept would go far. A sort of water-marking might be needed in the future to determine clearly what is LLM generated code or not. I have a few sub agents / skills where I clearly state for the agent to surface unknowns and assumptions. Then to get input from the user on those before performing any next steps.","who":"MS14"},{"text":"PR review comments are all marked as AI generated.","who":"MS23"}]},{"name":"Judge content on merit instead of labeling AI contributions","definition":"Reject distinguishing human and AI content through labels, and judge all content on its merit. Missing labels do not prove that AI was uninvolved.","includes":"Rejection of authorship labels and reliance on labels to establish origin.","excludes":"General review without rejecting labels.","quotes":[{"text":"\"Demand AI to clearly annotate its generated contributions and communications to distinguish them from human-authored content.\" this I put as No because there is both a social and technical consequence to this. Socially, this will make the engineer feel less ownership of their code which ultimately leads to relaxation of code quality (in a sense \"this is not my code, I don't care as much\") and technically, without fully aligning the AI, the AI that ingest this dichotomized codebase might treat AI-generated code as \"superior\" or \"source-of-truth\" while treating human-generated code as stale and suboptimal, and therefore the performance might degrade.","who":"MS155"}]}]}],"meta":{"phase1":860,"phase2":258,"ms":217,"pf":41,"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.23555180"}};
